window.PLAZA_FIGURES = [{"id":"osiel_cardenas_guillen","name":"Osiel Cárdenas Guillén","alias":"El Mata Amigos / El Loco","cartels":["gulf","zetas"],"born":1967,"died":0,"role":"Gulf Cartel boss who created Los Zetas as his private army","status_2026":"Released from US federal prison 30 Aug 2024, deported to Mexico 16 Dec 2024 and detained on arrival; held at the Altiplano maximum-security prison facing Mexican charges","bio":"Born 18 May 1967 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas, Cárdenas worked as a mechanic and briefly as a federal judicial police officer before entering the Gulf Cartel. In July 1999 he had his partner Salvador Gómez Herrera killed, earning the alias 'El Mata Amigos' (Friend-Killer), and took control of the organisation. Fearing rivals and the state, he hired Arturo Guzmán Decena, a deserter from the army's GAFE special forces, to recruit fellow commandos; the resulting armed wing, Los Zetas, became the template for cartel paramilitarism across Mexico. On 9 November 1999 he surrounded and threatened FBI and DEA agents on a Matamoros street, an incident that made him a US priority target. Mexican soldiers captured him after a firefight in Matamoros on 14 March 2003. He continued directing the cartel from prison until his extradition to the United States on 19 January 2007. He pleaded guilty in Houston and on 24 February 2010 received 25 years and a $50 million forfeiture. He completed the term at USP Terre Haute, was released to immigration custody on 30 August 2024, and was deported to Mexico on 16 December 2024, where he was detained immediately and sent to the Altiplano maximum-security prison outside Mexico City.","notable":["Founded Los Zetas around 1997-1999 by recruiting GAFE army deserters","Threatened US FBI and DEA agents at gunpoint in Matamoros, November 1999","Ran the Gulf Cartel from Mexican prison between 2003 and 2007","Sentenced in Houston to 25 years and $50 million forfeiture, February 2010","Deported back to Mexico and jailed at Altiplano in December 2024"],"state":"TAM","lat":25.8797,"lng":-97.5042,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osiel_C%C3%A1rdenas_Guill%C3%A9n","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osiel_C%C3%A1rdenas_Guill%C3%A9n","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"antonio_ezequiel_cardenas_guillen","name":"Antonio Ezequiel Cárdenas Guillén","alias":"Tony Tormenta","cartels":["gulf"],"born":1962,"died":2010,"role":"Co-leader of the Gulf Cartel after his brother Osiel's extradition","status_2026":"Killed 5 November 2010 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas","bio":"Born 5 March 1962 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas, Antonio Ezequiel Cárdenas Guillén was the younger brother of Gulf Cartel boss Osiel Cárdenas. He acquired the nickname 'Tony Tormenta' (Tony Storm) for his volatility. After Osiel's 2003 capture and 2007 extradition to the United States, he shared command of the Gulf Cartel with Jorge Eduardo 'El Coss' Costilla Sánchez, with Costilla handling strategy and political protection while Cárdenas represented the family's interests and controlled the lucrative Matamoros plaza on the Rio Grande. He was indicted in the United States on drug trafficking and money laundering counts, and the State Department offered a reward of up to $5 million for him. In early 2010 the Gulf Cartel broke violently with its former armed wing, Los Zetas, plunging Tamaulipas into open warfare and prompting a heavy federal deployment. On 5 November 2010, Mexican marines cornered him in Matamoros. More than 660 marines, seventeen vehicles and three helicopters were committed to an eight-hour battle that shut down the border city. The official toll was ten dead: Cárdenas, four of his gunmen, three marines, one soldier and the reporter Carlos Alberto Guajardo Romero. Local accounts put the real casualty figure far higher.","notable":["Shared Gulf Cartel command with 'El Coss' from 2007 to 2010","Controlled the Matamoros border crossing into Brownsville, Texas","Fought the 2010 Gulf Cartel war against Los Zetas","Killed 5 November 2010 in an eight-hour battle involving over 660 marines","Three marines, a soldier and journalist Carlos Alberto Guajardo also died"],"state":"TAM","lat":25.8797,"lng":-97.5042,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonio_Ezequiel_C%C3%A1rdenas_Guill%C3%A9n","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"jorge_eduardo_costilla_sanchez","name":"Jorge Eduardo Costilla Sánchez","alias":"El Coss","cartels":["gulf"],"born":1971,"died":0,"role":"Gulf Cartel leader who broke the alliance with Los Zetas in 2010","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the United States, life sentence imposed 22 September 2022","bio":"Born 6 January 1971 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas, Costilla Sánchez served as a municipal police officer before joining the Gulf Cartel, rising through its ranks as a protégé of Osiel Cárdenas. He was present during the November 1999 confrontation in which Cárdenas menaced US federal agents. When Cárdenas was jailed in 2003, Costilla assumed day-to-day command, later sharing power with Antonio Ezequiel 'Tony Tormenta' Cárdenas after the boss's 2007 extradition. Costilla specialised in bribery networks and political protection rather than battlefield command. In early 2010 he presided over the rupture between the Gulf Cartel and Los Zetas, its former enforcement wing, after Zetas gunmen killed a Gulf lieutenant and the Zetas refused to hand over the culprits; the split ignited a war that made Tamaulipas and Nuevo León among the deadliest states in Mexico. With Tony Tormenta dead in November 2010, Costilla became sole leader. Mexican marines arrested him without a shot fired in Tampico, Tamaulipas, on 12 September 2012. He was transferred to the United States on 30 September 2015, pleaded guilty on 26 September 2017 to conspiring to possess and distribute cocaine and marijuana and to assaulting federal agents, and on 22 September 2022 a federal court in Brownsville, Texas sentenced him to life imprisonment with a $5 million forfeiture.","notable":["Former municipal police officer who rose to lead the Gulf Cartel","Took part in the 1999 armed standoff with US agents in Matamoros","Presided over the 2010 Gulf Cartel split from Los Zetas","Captured without gunfire in Tampico, 12 September 2012","Sentenced to life in Brownsville, Texas, 22 September 2022"],"state":"TAM","lat":25.8797,"lng":-97.5042,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jorge_Eduardo_Costilla_S%C3%A1nchez","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"arturo_guzman_decena","name":"Arturo Guzmán Decena","alias":"Z-1","cartels":["zetas","gulf"],"born":1976,"died":2002,"role":"Army special forces deserter who founded Los Zetas","status_2026":"Killed 21 November 2002 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas","bio":"Born 13 January 1976 in Puebla, Guzmán Decena joined the Mexican Army and was selected for the Grupo Aeromóvil de Fuerzas Especiales (GAFE), an elite airmobile special forces unit trained in counter-insurgency, some of it by foreign instructors. He served in GAFE from about 1992 until 1997, reaching the rank of lieutenant, and was deployed in the army's operations in Chiapas following the 1994 Zapatista uprising. He deserted in 1997 and went to work for Osiel Cárdenas Guillén and the Gulf Cartel in Tamaulipas. Asked by Cárdenas to assemble the best armed men available, Guzmán Decena recruited some thirty-eight fellow deserters from GAFE and other army units, luring them with pay far above military wages. The unit took its name from the radio call sign 'Zeta'; as its commander he became Z-1 and assigned sequential Z numbers to his recruits. Los Zetas introduced military tactics, disciplined command structures, heavy weapons and systematic terror into Mexican drug trafficking, transforming cartel violence for a generation. Guzmán Decena was killed on 21 November 2002 when Mexican soldiers surprised him in Matamoros, Tamaulipas. A floral wreath later left at his grave read that he would always be in their hearts, signed 'from your family, Los Zetas'.","notable":["GAFE special forces lieutenant who deserted the army in 1997","Recruited about 38 military deserters to form Los Zetas","Originated the 'Z' numbering system used by the organisation","Introduced military doctrine and heavy weapons to cartel warfare","Shot dead by soldiers in Matamoros, 21 November 2002"],"state":"TAM","lat":25.8797,"lng":-97.5042,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arturo_Guzm%C3%A1n_Decena","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"heriberto_lazcano","name":"Heriberto Lazcano Lazcano","alias":"Z-3 / El Verdugo / El Lazca","cartels":["zetas","gulf"],"born":1974,"died":2012,"role":"Supreme leader of Los Zetas from 2004 until 2012","status_2026":"Killed 7 October 2012 in Progreso, Coahuila; his corpse was stolen from a funeral home","bio":"Born 25 December 1974 in Apan, Hidalgo, Lazcano enlisted in the Mexican Army at sixteen and was admitted to the GAFE special forces, receiving counter-insurgency and combat training. He deserted on 27 March 1998 and joined Arturo Guzmán Decena's founding cadre of Los Zetas, taking the call sign Z-3 and the nickname 'El Verdugo' (The Executioner) for his personal use of torture. After Guzmán Decena was killed in November 2002 command passed to Rogelio González Pizaña, 'Z-2'; when González Pizaña was captured in October 2004 Lazcano became undisputed leader. Under him, Los Zetas expanded from a Gulf Cartel enforcement wing into an autonomous criminal enterprise controlling territory in Coahuila, Nuevo León, Veracruz, Tabasco and into Guatemala, and diversifying into extortion, kidnapping, fuel theft and migrant smuggling. In early 2010 the Zetas formally broke with the Gulf Cartel, triggering a war across the northeast. His organisation was implicated in the August 2010 San Fernando massacre of 72 migrants and the 2011 Allende, Coahuila killings. On 7 October 2012 Mexican marines killed him in a shootout in Progreso, Coahuila; before authorities could complete forensic confirmation, armed men seized his body from a funeral home in nearby Sabinas, fuelling years of doubt about his death.","notable":["GAFE-trained soldier who became the third Zeta, Z-3","Took undisputed command of Los Zetas in 2004 after Z-2's capture","Turned the group from a Gulf enforcement wing into an independent cartel","Presided over the 2010 split with the Gulf Cartel and the San Fernando massacres","Body stolen from a Sabinas funeral home days after he was killed"],"state":"TAM","lat":27.4761,"lng":-99.5164,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heriberto_Lazcano","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"miguel_angel_trevino_morales","name":"Miguel Ángel Treviño Morales","alias":"Z-40","cartels":["zetas","gulf","cdn"],"born":1970,"died":0,"role":"Los Zetas leader notorious for extreme violence and mass killings","status_2026":"In US federal custody since his 27 February 2025 transfer from Mexico; no guilty plea or sentence publicly confirmed as of September 2026","bio":"Born 18 November 1970 in Nuevo Laredo, Tamaulipas, Treviño Morales grew up partly in Texas and began as a runner and enforcer for the Gulf Cartel in Nuevo Laredo in the late 1990s, recruited by Osiel Cárdenas for his English and his knowledge of the border. Although he had no military background he rose inside Los Zetas in the early 2000s, taking the call sign Z-40. He led the Zetas' brutal campaign to seize Nuevo Laredo from the Sinaloa Cartel and became second-in-command to Heriberto Lazcano, running the group's most violent operations. He is linked to the August 2010 San Fernando massacre of 72 Central and South American migrants, the 2011 discovery of nearly 200 bodies in mass graves in the same municipality, and the March 2011 destruction of Allende, Coahuila, where dozens of residents were abducted and killed in reprisal for a DEA informant leak. He was also known for burning victims alive in oil drums. After Lazcano was killed in October 2012, Treviño became supreme leader. Mexican marines captured him without a shot near Anáhuac, Nuevo León, outside Nuevo Laredo, on 15 July 2013. He remained imprisoned in Mexico until 27 February 2025, when Mexico handed him and 28 other prisoners to US authorities amid tariff pressure from Washington; he faces federal drug trafficking, money laundering and murder charges.","notable":["Rose from Nuevo Laredo street enforcer to supreme leader of Los Zetas","Linked to the 2010 San Fernando massacre of 72 migrants","Ordered the March 2011 Allende, Coahuila massacre after a DEA leak","Captured by Mexican marines near Nuevo Laredo, 15 July 2013","Sent to US custody with 28 other prisoners on 27 February 2025"],"state":"TAM","lat":27.4761,"lng":-99.5164,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miguel_Trevi%C3%B1o_Morales","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/"],"verified":true},{"id":"omar_trevino_morales","name":"Omar Treviño Morales","alias":"Z-42","cartels":["zetas","cdn"],"born":1974,"died":0,"role":"Last supreme leader of Los Zetas before the group fragmented","status_2026":"In US federal custody since his 27 February 2025 transfer from Mexico; no guilty plea or sentence publicly confirmed as of September 2026","bio":"Born 26 January 1974 in Nuevo Laredo, Tamaulipas, Omar Treviño Morales followed his older brother Miguel Ángel into the Gulf Cartel and then Los Zetas, taking the call sign Z-42. He oversaw operations in Coahuila and Nuevo León and was indicted in Washington, D.C. in 2008 over abductions and killings in Nuevo Laredo and over cocaine trafficking; the United States offered a reward of up to $5 million for him, and the Treasury Department sanctioned him under the Foreign Narcotics Kingpin Designation Act in 2010. When Miguel was captured in July 2013, Omar inherited a weakened and fracturing organisation. Under his brief leadership Los Zetas splintered, with the Treviño family's own faction eventually reconstituting itself as the Cártel del Noreste, based in Nuevo Laredo, while a rival wing became the Zetas Vieja Escuela. Federal police and army units arrested him without firing a shot at a house in San Pedro Garza García, an affluent suburb of Monterrey, Nuevo León, in the early hours of 4 March 2015. He was formally charged in Mexico on 13 March 2015 with money laundering and weapons offences and held in maximum security. On 27 February 2025 he was transferred to the United States alongside his brother and 27 other prisoners.","notable":["Took over Los Zetas after his brother Miguel's capture in July 2013","Presided over the fragmentation of Los Zetas into CDN and Vieja Escuela","Arrested in San Pedro Garza García, Nuevo León, 4 March 2015","US reward of $5 million and Kingpin Act sanctions in 2010","Transferred to US custody on 27 February 2025"],"state":"TAM","lat":27.4761,"lng":-99.5164,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omar_Trevi%C3%B1o_Morales","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":true},{"id":"arturo_beltran_leyva","name":"Marcos Arturo Beltrán Leyva","alias":"El Barbas / Jefe de Jefes","cartels":["beltran_leyva","sinaloa"],"born":1961,"died":2009,"role":"Founder and boss of the Beltrán Leyva Organization","status_2026":"Killed 16 December 2009 in Cuernavaca, Morelos","bio":"Born 27 September 1961 in Badiraguato, Sinaloa, Arturo Beltrán Leyva was the eldest of the brothers who rose within the Sinaloa Federation as close allies of Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán and Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada. He ran the Federation's corruption apparatus, cultivating police and political protection at the highest levels, and controlled trafficking corridors through Guerrero, Morelos, Nayarit and Sonora. The alliance collapsed after his brother Alfredo was arrested in Culiacán on 21 January 2008; Arturo blamed El Chapo for betraying him to the authorities. The Beltrán Leyva Organization broke away and allied with Los Zetas against Sinaloa, unleashing a war that devastated Sinaloa, Guerrero and Morelos and popularised the display of decapitated bodies and the hanging of banners. He recruited Edgar 'La Barbie' Valdez Villarreal and Sergio 'El Grande' Villarreal Barragán as enforcers. On 16 December 2009 about 200 Mexican marines, backed by two Mi-17 helicopters and armoured vehicles, besieged his luxury apartment complex in Cuernavaca, Morelos; after a ninety-minute firefight he and several gunmen were killed. Marine Melquisedec Angulo Córdova died in the raid, and gunmen murdered members of his family the day after his 21 December funeral in a notorious act of reprisal.","notable":["Ran the Sinaloa Federation's police and political corruption network","Broke with El Chapo in 2008 after his brother Alfredo's arrest","Allied the BLO with Los Zetas against the Sinaloa Cartel","Killed by about 200 marines in Cuernavaca, 16 December 2009","His organisation murdered the family of the marine killed in the raid"],"state":"MOR","lat":18.9186,"lng":-99.2342,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arturo_Beltr%C3%A1n_Leyva","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":true},{"id":"alfredo_beltran_leyva","name":"Alfredo Beltrán Leyva","alias":"El Mochomo","cartels":["beltran_leyva","sinaloa"],"born":1971,"died":0,"role":"Beltrán Leyva brother whose 2008 arrest split the Sinaloa Federation","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the US, life sentence, ADX Florence, Colorado","bio":"Born 21 January 1971 in La Palma, Badiraguato, Sinaloa, Alfredo Beltrán Leyva was nicknamed 'El Mochomo' after a desert ant. With his brothers Arturo, Héctor and Carlos he managed cocaine and methamphetamine trafficking for the Sinaloa Federation, overseeing shipments through Sinaloa and Sonora into Arizona and California, and handled much of the organisation's day-to-day logistics and bribery of state police. Mexican Army special forces arrested him in Culiacán, Sinaloa, on 21 January 2008 along with bodyguards, roughly $900,000 in cash, luxury watches and weapons. His brother Arturo concluded that Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán had informed on him in exchange for protection for his own son, and the Beltrán Leyva family split from the Federation. The rupture set off one of the bloodiest phases of the drug war, with Culiacán, Guerrero and Morelos turned into battlegrounds. Alfredo was extradited to the United States on 15 November 2014, appearing in a Washington, D.C. court two days later. He pleaded guilty before US District Judge Richard J. Leon on 23 February 2016, and was sentenced on 5 April 2017 to life imprisonment without parole and a $529 million forfeiture, calculated on at least 27.9 tons of narcotics shipped between 2000 and 2012. He was moved to the ADX Florence supermax in June 2022.","notable":["Arrest in Culiacán on 21 January 2008 triggered the Sinaloa-BLO war","Extradited to the United States on 15 November 2014","Pleaded guilty in Washington, D.C. on 23 February 2016","Sentenced to life without parole and $529 million forfeiture in April 2017","Transferred to ADX Florence supermax in June 2022"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7993,"lng":-107.3938,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfredo_Beltr%C3%A1n_Leyva","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfredo_Beltr%C3%A1n_Leyva","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/"],"verified":true},{"id":"hector_beltran_leyva","name":"Héctor Beltrán Leyva","alias":"El Ingeniero / El H","cartels":["beltran_leyva","sinaloa"],"born":1962,"died":2018,"role":"Led the Beltrán Leyva Organization after his brother Arturo's death","status_2026":"Died 18 November 2018 of a heart attack while in Mexican custody","bio":"Born 15 February 1962 in Badiraguato, Sinaloa, Héctor Beltrán Leyva was known as 'El Ingeniero' (The Engineer), 'El H' and 'El General'. He worked alongside his brothers in the Sinaloa Federation before the 2008 split, handling finance and logistics rather than street violence. After Arturo was killed by marines in Cuernavaca in December 2009, Héctor took over what remained of the Beltrán Leyva Organization, but the group had already begun fragmenting into rival successors including the Cártel del Pacífico Sur, the Cártel Independiente de Acapulco and Los Rojos, whose infighting turned Guerrero and Morelos into among the most violent regions of Mexico. Héctor kept a deliberately low profile, moving through central Mexico and reportedly living for years in San Miguel de Allende, Guanajuato, under a false identity as a businessman and art dealer. Mexican Army troops arrested him without resistance at a seafood restaurant in San Miguel de Allende on 1 October 2014 and transferred him on 6 October to the Altiplano maximum-security prison in Almoloya de Juárez. He remained in Mexican custody facing organised crime and money laundering charges. On 18 November 2018 he suffered chest pains and died of a heart attack at the Adolfo López Mateos medical centre in Toluca, aged 56.","notable":["Handled finance and logistics for the Beltrán Leyva Organization","Took command after Arturo was killed in December 2009","Lived under a false identity in San Miguel de Allende as an art dealer","Arrested without resistance at a restaurant on 1 October 2014","Died of a heart attack in custody in Toluca, 18 November 2018"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7993,"lng":-107.3938,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%A9ctor_Beltr%C3%A1n_Leyva","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":true},{"id":"edgar_valdez_villarreal","name":"Edgar Valdez Villarreal","alias":"La Barbie","cartels":["beltran_leyva","sinaloa"],"born":1973,"died":0,"role":"American-born enforcer who led the Beltrán Leyva Organization's hit squads","status_2026":"Sentenced 11 June 2018 to 49 years and one month; not listed in US Bureau of Prisons custody since November 2022, whereabouts undisclosed","bio":"Born 11 August 1973 in Laredo, Texas, Edgar Valdez Villarreal was a US citizen and high school football player whose fair hair and blue eyes prompted a coach to nickname him 'La Barbie'. After a marijuana indictment in Texas he fled to Mexico around 1998 and joined the Beltrán Leyva wing of the Sinaloa Federation, where he commanded Los Negros, a squad formed to fight Los Zetas for control of Nuevo Laredo and later Acapulco. He became one of the most visible symbols of the drug war's brutality, credited with popularising the filming of interrogations and executions and the public display of bodies. When the Beltrán Leyva Organization split from the Sinaloa Cartel in 2008, he ran its operations in Guerrero and Morelos. After Arturo Beltrán Leyva was killed in December 2009, Valdez fought Héctor Beltrán Leyva for succession, deepening the violence around Acapulco and Cuernavaca. Mexican federal police captured him near Mexico City on 30 August 2010. He was transferred to the United States on 30 September 2015, pleaded guilty in Atlanta to drug conspiracy and money laundering, and on 11 June 2018 was sentenced to 49 years and one month in federal prison. He was held at USP Coleman II in Florida but has not appeared in Bureau of Prisons records since November 2022.","notable":["US citizen from Laredo, Texas who became a Mexican cartel enforcer","Commanded Los Negros against Los Zetas in Nuevo Laredo and Acapulco","Helped popularise filmed executions and public body displays","Captured near Mexico City on 30 August 2010","Sentenced in Atlanta on 11 June 2018 to 49 years and one month"],"state":"GRO","lat":16.8531,"lng":-99.8237,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edgar_Valdez_Villarreal","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edgar_Valdez_Villarreal","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/"],"verified":true},{"id":"sergio_villarreal_barragan","name":"Sergio Enrique Villarreal Barragán","alias":"El Grande / El King","cartels":["beltran_leyva","sinaloa"],"born":1969,"died":0,"role":"Former police officer turned Beltrán Leyva lieutenant and US cooperating witness","status_2026":"In the United States; cooperated with US prosecutors and testified at the García Luna trial; current whereabouts undisclosed","bio":"Born 21 September 1969 in Torreón, Coahuila, Sergio Villarreal Barragán stood about 2.03 metres tall, earning him the nickname 'El Grande'. He joined the Coahuila state judicial police in 1990 and was admitted to the federal police in 1993, serving in Nuevo Laredo and Reynosa before moving into trafficking, first with the Juárez Cartel organisation and then with the Beltrán Leyva brothers within the Sinaloa Federation. He ran operations across Durango, Coahuila and the Laguna region and was among the senior figures who followed the Beltrán Leyva family when it broke from the Sinaloa Cartel in 2008. On 1 June 2010 the US Treasury sanctioned him under the Foreign Narcotics Kingpin Designation Act, freezing his American assets. After Arturo Beltrán Leyva's death in December 2009 he became one of several claimants to the organisation's remnants, in rivalry with Edgar 'La Barbie' Valdez Villarreal and Héctor Beltrán Leyva. Mexican marines arrested him in Puebla on 12 September 2010, reportedly without a shot fired. He was extradited to the United States on 23 May 2012 and agreed to cooperate with federal prosecutors, providing testimony about cartel corruption of Mexican officials, including at the 2023 Brooklyn trial of former security secretary Genaro García Luna. His current status and location have not been made public.","notable":["Former state and federal police officer who joined the Beltrán Leyva Organization","Sanctioned under the US Kingpin Act on 1 June 2010","Arrested by marines in Puebla, 12 September 2010","Extradited to the United States on 23 May 2012","Testified for the prosecution at Genaro García Luna's 2023 trial"],"state":"COA","lat":25.5428,"lng":-103.4068,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sergio_Villarreal_Barrag%C3%A1n","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"vicente_zambada_niebla","name":"Vicente Zambada Niebla","alias":"El Vicentillo","cartels":["sinaloa","mayiza"],"born":1975,"died":0,"role":"Sinaloa Cartel logistics chief and star US cooperating witness against El Chapo","status_2026":"Sentenced 2019 to 15 years with credit for time served since 2009; no longer listed in federal Bureau of Prisons custody, whereabouts undisclosed","bio":"Born 10 May 1975 in Culiacán, Sinaloa, Vicente Zambada Niebla is the son of Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada, the Sinaloa Cartel's long-serving co-founder. Known as 'El Vicentillo', he served as his father's logistics coordinator, arranging the movement of multi-ton cocaine loads from Colombia through Central America and Mexico using aircraft, submarines, speedboats and rail, and managing payments to corrupt officials. Mexican soldiers arrested him in Mexico City on 19 March 2009, hours after a meeting with DEA agents that became the basis of his later legal defence: he claimed the US government had granted the cartel's leadership immunity in exchange for intelligence, an argument the courts rejected. He was extradited to the United States in February 2010 and held in Chicago. He signed a cooperation agreement in 2013, agreeing to forfeit $1.37 billion, and in November 2018 became the prosecution's central witness at the Brooklyn trial of Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán, describing the cartel's structure, its bribery of Mexican officials and decades of shipments. On 30 May 2019 a federal judge sentenced him to 15 years, crediting his time in custody since 2009, and imposed a $4 million fine on top of the forfeiture. He is widely reported to have entered witness protection.","notable":["Son of Sinaloa co-founder Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada","Arrested in Mexico City on 19 March 2009 and extradited in February 2010","Argued unsuccessfully that the DEA had granted the cartel immunity","Star prosecution witness at El Chapo's 2018-2019 Brooklyn trial","Sentenced to 15 years on 30 May 2019 after agreeing to a $1.37 billion forfeiture"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7993,"lng":-107.3938,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vicente_Zambada_Niebla","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":true},{"id":"nazario_moreno_gonzalez","name":"Nazario Moreno González","alias":"El Chayo / El Más Loco / San Nazario","cartels":["familia_michoacana","templarios"],"born":1970,"died":2014,"role":"Messianic founder of La Familia Michoacana and the Knights Templar","status_2026":"Killed 9 March 2014 in Tumbiscatío, Michoacán, after being wrongly declared dead in 2010","bio":"Born 8 March 1970 in the Guanajuatillo ranchería of Apatzingán, Michoacán, Nazario Moreno González spent time in the United States before returning to Michoacán and entering the drug trade. He rose to the top of La Familia Michoacana alongside José de Jesús Méndez Vargas after Carlos Rosales Mendoza's capture in 2004. Moreno fused trafficking with a quasi-religious ideology, distributing his own book of pseudo-evangelical maxims, forbidding recruits from using the methamphetamine the group produced, and casting the cartel as a defender of Michoacán families. His organisation announced itself in September 2006 by rolling five severed heads onto a nightclub floor in Uruapan, an act that helped precipitate Felipe Calderón's militarised offensive that December. On 9 December 2010 federal police claimed to have killed him after a two-day gunfight at El Alcalde in Apatzingán, but no body was recovered; the government later conceded he may only have been wounded. He used the interval to build the Knights Templar Cartel as a splinter with elaborate rituals and codes while a cult of 'San Nazario' grew around shrines bearing his image. Mexican Army and Navy forces killed him on 9 March 2014 at Tumbiscatío, Michoacán, with two gunshot wounds to the thorax; his identity was confirmed by DNA and fingerprints.","notable":["Founded La Familia Michoacana and later the Knights Templar","Wrote a pseudo-religious code book distributed to cartel members","Announced the cartel with five severed heads in Uruapan, September 2006","Falsely declared killed by federal police in December 2010","Actually killed by the army and navy on 9 March 2014 in Tumbiscatío"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.0847,"lng":-102.3506,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nazario_Moreno_Gonz%C3%A1lez","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":true},{"id":"jose_de_jesus_mendez_vargas","name":"José de Jesús Méndez Vargas","alias":"El Chango","cartels":["familia_michoacana"],"born":1961,"died":0,"role":"La Familia Michoacana co-founder and last leader before the group split","status_2026":"In US custody after transfer from Mexico on 27 February 2025; facing federal drug trafficking charges in New York","bio":"Born 28 February 1961 in El Aguaje, Michoacán, José de Jesús Méndez Vargas was nicknamed 'El Chango' (The Monkey). He co-founded La Familia Michoacana with Nazario Moreno González and Servando 'La Tuta' Gómez, building an organisation that combined industrial-scale methamphetamine production with extortion of Michoacán's agriculture and commerce and a public rhetoric of social protection. The US Treasury sanctioned him under the Kingpin Act on 25 February 2010, freezing his American assets. When Moreno was reported killed in December 2010, Méndez claimed leadership, but his authority was immediately contested by Gómez and Enrique Plancarte, who broke away in early 2011 to form the Knights Templar Cartel. The resulting internal war shattered La Familia and left Méndez commanding a diminished faction. Mexican federal police arrested him at a road checkpoint in the state of Aguascalientes on 21 June 2011, effectively ending the original organisation; Mexico had offered a 30 million peso bounty for him. He was held in Mexican maximum security for well over a decade; a Mexican federal court rejected his challenge to extradition on 8 April 2014, but the transfer stalled for years. On 27 February 2025 he was among 29 prisoners handed to US authorities, and he faces drug trafficking charges in a New York federal court.","notable":["Co-founded La Familia Michoacana with Nazario Moreno and La Tuta","Sanctioned under the US Kingpin Act on 25 February 2010","Lost control of the cartel when the Knights Templar split off in 2011","Arrested at a checkpoint in Aguascalientes, 21 June 2011","Transferred to US custody on 27 February 2025"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.0847,"lng":-102.3506,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_de_Jes%C3%BAs_M%C3%A9ndez_Vargas","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":true},{"id":"servando_gomez_martinez","name":"Servando Gómez Martínez","alias":"La Tuta","cartels":["familia_michoacana","templarios"],"born":1966,"died":0,"role":"Schoolteacher turned Knights Templar leader and media propagandist","status_2026":"In US custody after being sent from Mexico on 12 August 2025; previously sentenced in Mexico to 55 years in June 2019","bio":"Born 6 February 1966 in Arteaga, Michoacán, Servando Gómez Martínez trained and worked as a primary schoolteacher, remaining on the Michoacán education payroll into 2010, which gave him the alias 'La Tuta' (The Teacher). He co-founded La Familia Michoacana in 2006 and served as its operational chief and public voice, giving telephone interviews to radio and television and filming videos in which he cast the cartel as a protector of Michoacán against outsiders. After Nazario Moreno's reported death in 2010, Gómez broke with José de Jesús Méndez and founded the Knights Templar Cartel with Enrique Plancarte in early 2011. Under his leadership the group extended extortion across Michoacán's lime, avocado, cattle and iron ore industries and took control of the port of Lázaro Cárdenas, provoking the 2013 rise of the autodefensa militias led by José Manuel Mireles and Hipólito Mora. Videos surfaced in 2014 showing him meeting politicians and journalists, deepening a corruption scandal. Federal police arrested him before dawn on 27 February 2015 at a house in Morelia, after tracking a birthday cake ordered by his girlfriend. In June 2019 a Mexican court sentenced him to 55 years for a 2011 kidnapping. On 12 August 2025 Mexico sent him to the United States, where he faces cocaine and methamphetamine trafficking charges.","notable":["Former primary schoolteacher, hence the alias 'La Tuta'","Co-founded La Familia Michoacana and then the Knights Templar","Ran extortion of Michoacán's lime, avocado and iron ore economies","Arrested in Morelia on 27 February 2015; sentenced to 55 years in 2019","Sent to US custody on 12 August 2025"],"state":"MIC","lat":18.3833,"lng":-102.2833,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Servando_G%C3%B3mez_Mart%C3%ADnez","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/"],"verified":true},{"id":"enrique_plancarte_solis","name":"Enrique Plancarte Solís","alias":"Kike","cartels":["familia_michoacana","templarios"],"born":1970,"died":2014,"role":"Knights Templar co-founder who ran methamphetamine production","status_2026":"Killed 31 March 2014 in Colón, Querétaro","bio":"Born 14 September 1970 in Nueva Italia, Michoacán, Enrique Plancarte Solís was a senior figure in La Familia Michoacana before helping create the Knights Templar Cartel in early 2011 alongside Servando 'La Tuta' Gómez, following Nazario Moreno's reported death. Within both organisations his principal responsibility was methamphetamine: coordinating the import of precursor chemicals through the Pacific port of Lázaro Cárdenas, running clandestine laboratories in the Michoacán highlands, and organising smuggling routes into the United States. The US Treasury Department designated him under the Foreign Narcotics Kingpin Designation Act on 25 February 2010, and both Mexican and American authorities placed substantial rewards on him. He was also linked to the Knights Templar's extortion of agriculture and mining and to its control of local politics in the Tierra Caliente. As the autodefensa uprising and a federal offensive rolled back the cartel across Michoacán during 2013 and early 2014, Plancarte fled the state. Mexican Navy personnel killed him in a firefight in Colón, Querétaro, on 31 March 2014, three weeks after Nazario Moreno was killed. His daughter, the singer Melissa Plancarte, publicly acknowledged him as her father that year; several relatives were arrested in the months that followed.","notable":["Co-founded the Knights Templar Cartel with La Tuta in 2011","Coordinated methamphetamine production and precursor imports","Sanctioned under the US Kingpin Act on 25 February 2010","Killed by the Mexican Navy in Colón, Querétaro, 31 March 2014","His daughter is the Mexican singer Melissa Plancarte"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.0167,"lng":-102.1,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Plancarte_Sol%C3%ADs","https://insightcrime.org/criminal-profiles/"],"verified":true},{"id":"armando_valencia_cornelio","name":"Armando Valencia Cornelio","alias":"Maradona","cartels":["milenio"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Founder of the Milenio (Valencia) Cartel, the organisation CJNG grew out of","status_2026":"Reported released early from Mexican prison by around July 2020 and said to be seriously ill; no confirmed public status since","bio":"Armando Valencia Cornelio led the Valencia family organisation known as the Milenio Cartel, rooted in Aguililla and the Michoacán Tierra Caliente. The family began as agricultural producers before moving into cannabis and opium poppy cultivation in Michoacán and Jalisco in the late 1970s, initially selling to larger trafficking groups. Under Valencia Cornelio the group built direct relationships with Colombian suppliers during the mid-1990s, and by the early 2000s had become a significant cocaine transporter and an early mover into synthetic drugs, operating largely out of Guadalajara, Jalisco. The organisation worked in alliance with the Sinaloa Federation, particularly with Ignacio 'Nacho' Coronel Villarreal, functioning in effect as a Sinaloa branch. Mexican authorities captured Valencia Cornelio on 15 August 2003, and leadership passed to Luis Valencia Valencia and Óscar Nava Valencia. After Nava Valencia's capture in 2009 and Coronel's death in 2010 the Milenio Cartel fractured into rival successors, principally La Resistencia and the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación under Nemesio 'El Mencho' Oseguera Cervantes, whose turf war reshaped Jalisco and Michoacán; CJNG is now among the most powerful criminal organisations in the hemisphere. Valencia Cornelio was reported to have been released early from prison by around July 2020 and to be suffering from cancer; no authoritative account of his situation has been published since.","notable":["Built the Michoacán-based Milenio or Valencia Cartel","Established direct cocaine supply links with Colombian traffickers","Operated as a branch of the Sinaloa Federation under Nacho Coronel","Captured on 15 August 2003, ending his active leadership","The cartel's 2009-2010 collapse produced La Resistencia and the CJNG"],"state":"MIC","lat":18.7333,"lng":-102.7833,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milenio_Cartel","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"teodoro_garcia_simental","name":"Teodoro García Simental","alias":"El Teo / Tres Letras","cartels":["tijuana","sinaloa"],"born":1974,"died":0,"role":"Tijuana Cartel commander whose 2008 defection set off the battle for Tijuana","status_2026":"Imprisoned in Mexico since his January 2010 capture; no confirmed transfer to US custody","bio":"Born in 1974 in Culiacán, Sinaloa, Teodoro García Simental began working for the Tijuana Cartel around 1995 with his brother Marco Antonio, serving as an enforcer under Ramón Arellano Félix. As the Arellano Félix Organization weakened through successive arrests, García built an autonomous network in eastern Tijuana funded heavily by kidnapping for ransom and extortion rather than trafficking alone. In April 2008 a power struggle with the family's new leader, Luis Fernando Sánchez Arellano, erupted into open fighting in Tijuana; García defected and forged an alliance with the Sinaloa Cartel. The rupture, compounded by the capture of Eduardo Arellano Félix that October, turned Tijuana into one of the drug war's most violent theatres through 2008 and 2009, with bodies hung from bridges and near-daily gun battles. His organisation became notorious for the work of Santiago Meza López, 'El Pozolero' (The Stew Maker), who confessed to dissolving more than three hundred corpses in caustic soda on García's orders. The Mexican government offered a reward of about two million dollars for him. Federal police captured him on 12 January 2010 at a residence in La Paz, Baja California Sur. He has since been held in Mexican maximum-security prison on organised crime, kidnapping and homicide charges.","notable":["Defected from the Tijuana Cartel to the Sinaloa Cartel in April 2008","His split triggered the 2008-2009 battle for Tijuana","Employed Santiago Meza, 'El Pozolero', who dissolved over 300 bodies","Built a criminal economy centred on kidnapping and extortion","Captured in La Paz, Baja California Sur, 12 January 2010"],"state":"BCN","lat":32.5149,"lng":-117.0382,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teodoro_Garc%C3%ADa_Simental","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"zhenli_ye_gon","name":"Zhenli Ye Gon","alias":"","cartels":["other"],"born":1963,"died":0,"role":"Chinese-Mexican pharmaceutical importer at the centre of the largest cash seizure in drug-war history","status_2026":"Held in Mexico since his October 2016 extradition; no final conviction publicly confirmed","bio":"Born 31 January 1963 in Shanghai, Zhenli Ye Gon emigrated to Mexico and became a naturalised Mexican citizen in 2002. He owned Unimed Pharm Chem México, a company licensed to import pharmaceutical chemicals, and built a plant in Toluca, Estado de México. Mexican and US authorities alleged the firm was a front for importing pseudoephedrine and other methamphetamine precursors from Asia on an industrial scale, feeding the synthetic drug boom that reshaped Mexican organised crime in the 2000s. On 15 March 2007 Mexican federal agents raided his mansion in the Lomas de Chapultepec district of Mexico City and seized roughly $207 million in US cash, along with 18 million pesos, 200,000 euros, jewellery and weapons; it remains the largest cash seizure ever made in a drug investigation. Ye Gon fled to the United States and was arrested at a restaurant in Wheaton, Maryland, on 23 July 2007. He claimed the money belonged to Mexican political figures who had coerced him into storing it, an allegation never substantiated. US prosecutors dismissed all charges with prejudice on 28 August 2009 after a key witness recanted. After years of litigation he was extradited to Mexico on 18 October 2016 to face organised crime and drug charges and has remained in Mexican custody since.","notable":["Seizure of about $207 million in cash at his Mexico City home, March 2007","Owner of Unimed Pharm Chem México, accused of importing meth precursors","Arrested in Maryland in July 2007; US charges dismissed in August 2009","Extradited from the United States to Mexico on 18 October 2016","Claimed the cash belonged to Mexican politicians, never substantiated"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1332,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhenli_Ye_Gon","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhenli_Ye_Gon"],"verified":true},{"id":"felipe_calderon","name":"Felipe Calderón Hinojosa","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1962,"died":0,"role":"President of Mexico 2006-2012 who launched the militarised war on the cartels","status_2026":"Alive; private citizen, never charged; under sustained public pressure over García Luna's conviction","bio":"Born 18 August 1962 in Morelia, Michoacán, Felipe Calderón was a career National Action Party politician who won the 2006 presidential election by a margin of less than one percent amid disputed results. Seeking to establish authority, he made public security his defining project. Within days of taking office on 1 December 2006 he launched Operativo Conjunto Michoacán, announced on 11 December, deploying thousands of soldiers and federal police to his home state against La Familia Michoacana, and subsequently extended military operations to Baja California, Chihuahua, Tamaulipas and Guerrero. His strategy centred on capturing or killing cartel leaders, a 'kingpin' approach that removed many bosses but fragmented organisations into more numerous and often more violent successors. Homicides rose sharply through his term, with credible estimates of between sixty thousand and one hundred thousand drug-related deaths and tens of thousands of disappearances. His government also negotiated the Mérida Initiative with the United States. He left office on 30 November 2012 and took a fellowship at the Harvard Kennedy School. The February 2023 US conviction of his security secretary Genaro García Luna for taking Sinaloa Cartel bribes intensified calls in Mexico for him to be investigated; he has denied any knowledge of the corruption and has not been charged.","notable":["Launched Operativo Conjunto Michoacán in December 2006, opening the drug war","Pursued a kingpin-capture strategy that fragmented the cartels","Negotiated the Mérida Initiative security package with Washington","Tens of thousands of drug-related deaths occurred during his six-year term","Faced calls for investigation after García Luna's 2023 US conviction"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.706,"lng":-101.195,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felipe_Calder%C3%B3n","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"genaro_garcia_luna","name":"Genaro García Luna","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1968,"died":0,"role":"Calderón's security secretary, convicted in the US of taking Sinaloa Cartel bribes","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the US, 38-year sentence imposed 16 October 2024, held at ADX Florence","bio":"Born 10 July 1968 in Mexico City, Genaro García Luna trained as a mechanical engineer and rose through Mexico's intelligence service before being appointed founding director of the Agencia Federal de Investigación in November 2001 under Vicente Fox. In December 2006 Felipe Calderón made him Secretary of Public Security, the post from which he directed the federal police component of the drug war until 2012, becoming Washington's principal Mexican counterpart and a conduit for hundreds of millions of dollars in Mérida Initiative assistance. After leaving office he moved to Florida and ran security consultancies. US prosecutors in Brooklyn indicted him in December 2019, alleging he had accepted multimillion-dollar cash bribes from the Sinaloa Cartel in exchange for protecting its shipments, tipping it off about operations and steering enforcement against its rivals. Witnesses at trial included Jesús 'El Rey' Zambada and Sergio Villarreal Barragán. On 21 February 2023 a jury convicted him on all five counts, including engaging in a continuing criminal enterprise and cocaine trafficking conspiracy. On 16 October 2024 he was sentenced to 38 years in federal prison and is held at ADX Florence, Colorado, with a projected release date in 2052. On 22 May 2025 a Florida court ordered him and his wife to pay about $2.448 billion in damages to the Mexican government.","notable":["Founding director of the AFI, 2001-2006, then security secretary 2006-2012","Principal US counterpart during the Mérida Initiative","Convicted in Brooklyn on all five counts on 21 February 2023","Sentenced on 16 October 2024 to 38 years, held at ADX Florence","Ordered on 22 May 2025 to pay Mexico about $2.448 billion in damages"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1332,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genaro_Garc%C3%ADa_Luna","https://www.bop.gov/inmateloc/","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"vicente_fox","name":"Vicente Fox Quesada","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1942,"died":0,"role":"President of Mexico 2000-2006 whose term preceded the militarised drug war","status_2026":"Alive; retired at his Guanajuato ranch, prominent advocate of drug legalisation","bio":"Born 2 July 1942 in Mexico City and raised on a ranch in Guanajuato, Vicente Fox was a Coca-Cola executive and Guanajuato governor before winning the July 2000 presidential election for the National Action Party, ending seventy-one years of continuous rule by the Institutional Revolutionary Party. That political rupture also disrupted the informal arrangements through which the old ruling party had regulated drug trafficking, contributing to the fragmentation and competition among cartels that followed. Fox created the Agencia Federal de Investigación under Genaro García Luna in 2001 and used the army against traffickers, particularly after Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán escaped from Puente Grande prison in January 2001, weeks after Fox took office. His government captured senior figures including Benjamín Arellano Félix in March 2002 and Osiel Cárdenas Guillén in March 2003, and extradited record numbers of suspects to the United States. Violence nonetheless climbed sharply in Nuevo Laredo, Michoacán and Baja California during his final years, and in 2005 he deployed federal forces under Operación México Seguro, a precursor to Calderón's offensive. He left office on 30 November 2006. In retirement at Rancho San Cristóbal in Guanajuato he became an outspoken critic of prohibition, calling for the legalisation of cannabis and other drugs.","notable":["Ended 71 years of PRI rule with his 2000 election victory","El Chapo escaped from Puente Grande weeks into his term, January 2001","Oversaw the capture of Benjamín Arellano Félix and Osiel Cárdenas","Launched Operación México Seguro in 2005, a precursor to Calderón's war","Became a leading advocate of drug legalisation after leaving office"],"state":"GUA","lat":21.0189,"lng":-101.8597,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vicente_Fox","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"enrique_pena_nieto","name":"Enrique Peña Nieto","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1966,"died":0,"role":"President of Mexico 2012-2018 who recaptured El Chapo twice","status_2026":"Alive; reported to be living in Spain, named in corruption investigations but not charged","bio":"Born 20 July 1966 in Atlacomulco, Estado de México, Enrique Peña Nieto governed his home state from 2005 to 2011 before returning the Institutional Revolutionary Party to the presidency on 1 December 2012. He campaigned on shifting attention away from the drug war toward economic reform, and his government rhetorically de-emphasised cartel violence while structurally continuing Calderón's approach, creating a Gendarmería division within the Federal Police in 2014 and relying heavily on the marines. His term produced spectacular captures, including Miguel Ángel Treviño Morales in July 2013, Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán in February 2014, Héctor Beltrán Leyva in October 2014 and Servando Gómez in February 2015, but also catastrophic failures: Guzmán tunnelled out of Altiplano prison in July 2015 before being recaptured in January 2016 and extradited in January 2017. The September 2014 disappearance of 43 students from Ayotzinapa in Iguala, Guerrero, and the government's discredited 'historical truth' account of it, became the defining scandal of his presidency, alongside the 2014 Tlatlaya army killings and the Casa Blanca conflict-of-interest affair. Homicides reached record highs in his final year. Since leaving office on 30 November 2018 he has lived largely abroad, reportedly in Spain, and has been named in Odebrecht and campaign-finance investigations without being charged.","notable":["Oversaw the 2014 capture, 2015 escape and 2016 recapture of El Chapo","Extradited El Chapo to the United States in January 2017","Presided over the Ayotzinapa disappearances and the 'historical truth' scandal","Homicides reached then-record levels by the end of his term","Named in Odebrecht and campaign-finance probes; not charged"],"state":"MEX","lat":19.2826,"lng":-99.6557,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Pe%C3%B1a_Nieto","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"jose_manuel_mireles","name":"José Manuel Mireles Valverde","alias":"El Doctor","cartels":["autodefensas"],"born":1958,"died":2020,"role":"Physician who led the Michoacán autodefensa uprising against the Knights Templar","status_2026":"Died 25 November 2020 of COVID-19","bio":"Born 24 October 1958 in Tepalcatepec, Michoacán, José Manuel Mireles Valverde practised as a physician in his home town in the Tierra Caliente. After years of Knights Templar extortion, kidnappings and killings touching his own family, he helped launch an armed self-defence movement on 24 February 2013 in Tepalcatepec, the same day a parallel uprising began in La Ruana under Hipólito Mora. Articulate and media-savvy, Mireles became the movement's national face, giving interviews explaining that citizens had taken up arms because the state would not protect them. Through 2013 the autodefensas expanded across dozens of Michoacán municipalities, expelling the Knights Templar from towns the federal government had failed to secure and contributing directly to the cartel's collapse. He survived a plane crash in January 2014 that he believed was an attempt on his life. When the government moved in 2014 to absorb the militias into a state Rural Defence Force, Mireles refused to disarm. Federal forces detained him with dozens of followers in Lázaro Cárdenas on 27 June 2014 on firearms charges. He spent roughly three years in prison, was released on parole on 11 May 2017 and acquitted in 2018. He later took a post in the federal ISSSTE health system and died of COVID-19 on 25 November 2020.","notable":["Physician who co-launched the Michoacán autodefensas on 24 February 2013","Became the movement's most prominent national spokesman","Autodefensas helped destroy the Knights Templar across Michoacán","Jailed on weapons charges 27 June 2014, paroled May 2017, acquitted 2018","Died of COVID-19 on 25 November 2020, aged 62"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.1878,"lng":-102.8461,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_Manuel_Mireles_Valverde","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"hipolito_mora","name":"Hipólito Mora Chávez","alias":"","cartels":["autodefensas"],"born":1955,"died":2023,"role":"Lime grower who founded the first Michoacán autodefensa group in La Ruana","status_2026":"Assassinated 29 June 2023 in La Ruana, Michoacán","bio":"Born 26 July 1955 in Buenavista Tomatlán, Michoacán, Hipólito Mora was a lime grower in La Ruana. He had twice been imprisoned in the United States for drug offences, first after an arrest on 22 June 1989 and again in 1993, and was deported in 1995. On 24 February 2013, after Knights Templar operatives controlling the local lime packing houses squeezed growers, Mora took his pistol to confront them and rallied neighbours into an armed self-defence group, the first of the Michoacán autodefensas, launched the same day as Mireles's group in Tepalcatepec. The movement spread rapidly and broke the Knights Templar's hold over the Tierra Caliente. Mora was jailed briefly in 2014 over a factional killing and released for lack of evidence. On 16 December 2014 a gun battle in La Ruana between his fighters and a rival autodefensa faction led by Luis Antonio Torres, 'El Americano', left eleven dead including his son Manuel. Mora later joined the government-sanctioned Rural Defence Force, ran unsuccessfully for local office and then for governor of Michoacán in 2021, finishing fifth with about 3 percent of the vote, and continued denouncing the collusion of officials with Cárteles Unidos. On 29 June 2023 gunmen intercepted his convoy in La Ruana, opened fire and set his armoured vehicle alight, killing him and three bodyguards.","notable":["Founded the first Michoacán autodefensa group in La Ruana, 24 February 2013","Lime grower who armed neighbours against Knights Templar extortion","His son Manuel was killed in a December 2014 clash between militia factions","Ran for governor of Michoacán in 2021, finishing fifth","Assassinated with three bodyguards in La Ruana, 29 June 2023"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.1428,"lng":-102.5464,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hip%C3%B3lito_Mora","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"],"verified":true},{"id":"jose_luis_abarca","name":"José Luis Abarca Velázquez","alias":"","cartels":["guerreros_unidos"],"born":1961,"died":0,"role":"Mayor of Iguala accused of ordering the attack that led to the Ayotzinapa disappearances","status_2026":"Imprisoned in Mexico since November 2014, held at the Altiplano prison on organised crime, money laundering and illicit enrichment charges","bio":"Born 18 March 1961 in Arcelia, Guerrero, José Luis Abarca Velázquez was a jewellery and shopping-centre businessman in Iguala, Guerrero, who served as the city's municipal president from 1 October 2012 until 4 October 2014, elected on a Party of the Democratic Revolution ticket. Prosecutors alleged that he and his wife, María de los Ángeles Pineda Villa, whose brothers had been Beltrán Leyva operatives, were tied to the Guerreros Unidos organisation, which had emerged from the collapse of the Beltrán Leyva network and dominated Iguala's heroin trade and municipal police. On the night of 26-27 September 2014, municipal police and gunmen attacked buses commandeered by students from the Ayotzinapa rural teachers' college; six people were killed, dozens injured, and 43 students were seized and disappeared. Federal authorities initially accused Abarca of ordering the students detained to stop them disrupting an event featuring his wife, a version later discredited along with the government's broader 'historical truth'. The couple fled and were captured in the Iztapalapa district of Mexico City on 4 November 2014, and Abarca was sent the next day to the Altiplano maximum-security prison. A 2016 federal ruling confirmed his formal imprisonment for organised crime, money laundering and illicit enrichment, the charges on which he is still held; the Ayotzinapa case against him has never been resolved.","notable":["Mayor of Iguala, Guerrero, from October 2012 to October 2014","Accused of ties to the Guerreros Unidos through his wife's family","Iguala police attacked Ayotzinapa students on 26-27 September 2014, 43 disappeared","Captured with his wife in Mexico City on 4 November 2014","Held at Altiplano on organised crime, money laundering and illicit enrichment charges"],"state":"GRO","lat":18.3447,"lng":-99.5397,"sources":["https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_Luis_Abarca_Vel%C3%A1zquez","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_Luis_Abarca_Vel%C3%A1zquez","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iguala_mass_kidnapping"],"verified":true},{"id":"miguel_angel_felix_gallardo","name":"Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo","alias":"El Padrino / El Jefe de Jefes","cartels":["guadalajara","sinaloa"],"born":1946,"died":0,"role":"Founder and boss of the Guadalajara Cartel, architect of Mexico's first national trafficking federation","status_2026":"Aged 80 and reported in poor health. Imprisoned in Mexico since 1989, held since December 2014 in a medium-security federal prison in Jalisco (Puente Grande). A Mexican court granted him house arrest on 12 September 2022, but the transfer was reported suspended and later accounts indicate it was never carried out; his precise custodial status in 2026 is not independently confirmed.","bio":"Born 8 January 1946 in Culiacán, Sinaloa, Félix Gallardo served in the Sinaloa state judicial police and as a bodyguard to governor Leopoldo Sánchez Celis, ties that later shielded his trafficking. After the army's Operation Condor pushed Sinaloan growers out of the sierra, he moved to Guadalajara around 1980 and, with Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo and Rafael Caro Quintero, assembled the Guadalajara Cartel — the first Mexican organization to move Colombian cocaine at industrial scale for the Medellín Cartel, brokered through Honduran partner Juan Ramón Matta-Ballesteros. Known as 'El Padrino', he bought protection from the Dirección Federal de Seguridad, whose credentials his lieutenants carried. The February 1985 kidnapping, torture and murder of DEA agent Enrique Camarena shattered that arrangement; under sustained US pressure, federal commander Guillermo González Calderoni arrested him in Guadalajara on 8 April 1989. Mexican accounts hold that from prison he parcelled out the border plazas — Tijuana to his Arellano Félix relatives, Ciudad Juárez to Rafael Aguilar Guajardo and Amado Carrillo Fuentes, the Sonoran corridor to the Caro Quintero brothers, and the Sinaloa Pacific routes to Zambada, Guzmán and Palma — a division that seeded the modern cartel map. Sentenced to 40 years in the Camarena case, he received a further 37-year term at a 2017 retrial.","notable":["Assembled the Guadalajara Cartel around 1980, uniting Sinaloan traffickers with Colombian cocaine supply","Bought systematic protection from the DFS, Mexico's Cold War political police","Arrested 8 April 1989 in Guadalajara for the murder of DEA agent Enrique Camarena","Credited with dividing the border into 'plazas', the template for later cartel territories","Sentenced to 40 years, plus an additional 37 years at a 2017 retrial; moved from Altiplano to a Jalisco prison in December 2014"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miguel_%C3%81ngel_F%C3%A9lix_Gallardo","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guadalajara_Cartel","https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/mexican-drug-lord-who-was-jailed-killing-us-agent-granted-house-arrest-2022-09-12/","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/rafael-caro-quintero/"],"verified":true},{"id":"rafael_caro_quintero","name":"Rafael Caro Quintero","alias":"El Narco de Narcos / RCQ","cartels":["guadalajara","caborca","sinaloa"],"born":1952,"died":0,"role":"Guadalajara Cartel co-founder, marijuana magnate, and prime suspect in the murder of DEA agent Enrique Camarena","status_2026":"In US federal custody in the Eastern District of New York (Brooklyn) after his transfer from Mexico on 27 February 2025; pleaded not guilty at his 28 February 2025 arraignment. Reporting that the Justice Department declined to seek the death penalty in August 2025, and that plea talks were under way in 2026 with trial set for 2027, could not be independently confirmed here.","bio":"Born 24 October 1952 in La Noria, Badiraguato, Sinaloa, Caro Quintero rose from a peasant marijuana-growing family to become the Guadalajara Cartel's most flamboyant partner alongside Félix Gallardo and Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo. His signature venture was Rancho Búfalo in Allende, Chihuahua, an industrial marijuana plantation of roughly 1,000 hectares worked by thousands of labourers, which Mexican soldiers destroyed in November 1984 after DEA intelligence — widely attributed to agent Enrique Camarena — exposed it. In revenge, gunmen seized Camarena and pilot Alfredo Zavala Avelar in Guadalajara on 7 February 1985; Camarena was tortured for some 30 hours at a Caro Quintero property on Calle Lope de Vega and killed. Caro Quintero fled to Costa Rica with his girlfriend Sara Cosío and was arrested in Alajuela on 4 April 1985, then returned to Mexico, where he was convicted and sentenced to 40 years. A state appellate court freed him on a jurisdictional technicality on 9 August 2013 after 28 years inside; the Supreme Court quickly voided the ruling but he had vanished, rebuilding influence in Sonora through the so-called Caborca Cartel. Recaptured on 15 July 2022 at San Simón, in Choix municipality, Sinaloa, he was held at Altiplano until Mexico sent him and 28 others to US custody in February 2025.","notable":["Ran Rancho Búfalo in Allende, Chihuahua, a marijuana plantation of roughly 1,000 hectares destroyed by the army in November 1984","Prime suspect in the 1985 abduction, torture and murder of DEA agent Enrique 'Kiki' Camarena","Released on a technicality on 9 August 2013 and a fugitive for nine years on FBI and DEA most-wanted lists","Recaptured 15 July 2022 at San Simón, Choix, Sinaloa; a navy Black Hawk supporting the operation crashed, killing 14 of the 15 aboard","Sent to the United States on 27 February 2025 in a transfer of 29 prisoners and arraigned in Brooklyn the next day"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rafael_Caro_Quintero","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/rafael-caro-quintero/","https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Camarena_(DEA_agent)"],"verified":true},{"id":"ernesto_fonseca_carrillo","name":"Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo","alias":"Don Neto","cartels":["guadalajara"],"born":1930,"died":0,"role":"Guadalajara Cartel co-founder and senior partner; uncle and mentor of Amado Carrillo Fuentes","status_2026":"Alive and free in Mexico at 96. Released in April 2025 after completing his 40-year sentence, having spent the final years under house arrest in Estado de México. He remains under US indictment.","bio":"Born 1 August 1930 in Badiraguato, Sinaloa (the DEA long listed 1942), Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo was the oldest of the Guadalajara triumvirate and the bridge between the old sierra opium trade and the cocaine era. He trafficked heroin and marijuana from the 1960s, survived Operation Condor by relocating to Guadalajara, and there partnered with Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo and Rafael Caro Quintero around 1980 to move Colombian cocaine north. His nephews Amado and Vicente Carrillo Fuentes learned the business under him, as did much of the later Juárez organization. Fonseca was implicated in the February 1985 kidnapping and murder of DEA agent Enrique Camarena — a recorded interrogation of the agent was made at properties tied to the group. The Mexican Army surrounded his villa in Puerto Vallarta, Jalisco, on 7 April 1985, days after Caro Quintero's capture in Costa Rica, and he surrendered. Convicted of the Camarena killing and related charges, he received a 40-year sentence, serving most of it at Almoloya/Altiplano and Puente Grande before being moved in July 2016 to house arrest in Atizapán, Estado de México, on grounds of age and illness, with about nine years left to serve. He completed the sentence and was formally freed in April 2025, aged 94.","notable":["Oldest of the three Guadalajara Cartel founders and a veteran of the 1960s-70s Sinaloa heroin trade","Uncle and mentor of Amado Carrillo Fuentes, later the Juárez Cartel's 'Lord of the Skies'","Surrendered to the army on 7 April 1985 in Puerto Vallarta over the Camarena murder; sentenced to 40 years","Transferred to house arrest in July 2016 because of age and failing health, with roughly nine years of sentence remaining","Completed his sentence and walked free in April 2025 at 94, the last of the Guadalajara founders to go free"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ernesto_Fonseca_Carrillo","https://www.excelsior.com.mx/nacional/2016/07/28/1107610","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guadalajara_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Camarena_(DEA_agent)"],"verified":true},{"id":"enrique_camarena","name":"Enrique Camarena Salazar","alias":"Kiki","cartels":[],"born":1947,"died":1985,"role":"US DEA special agent in Guadalajara whose 1985 murder transformed US-Mexico drug policy","status_2026":"Killed February 1985; commemorated annually by Red Ribbon Week; Rafael Caro Quintero awaits trial in Brooklyn for his murder","bio":"Born 26 July 1947 in Mexicali, Baja California, and raised in Calexico, California, Enrique 'Kiki' Camarena served in the US Marine Corps and worked as a police officer and investigator before joining the Drug Enforcement Administration in 1974. After postings in Calexico and Fresno he was assigned to the DEA's Guadalajara resident office in 1980, where he proved unusually effective at recruiting informants inside the Guadalajara Cartel. His intelligence underpinned the November 1984 army raid that destroyed Rancho Búfalo, a roughly 1,000-hectare marijuana plantation in Allende, Chihuahua, linked to Rafael Caro Quintero and valued in the billions of dollars. On 7 February 1985 gunmen — including men carrying Dirección Federal de Seguridad credentials — abducted Camarena in broad daylight near the US consulate in Guadalajara; Mexican pilot and DEA source Alfredo Zavala Avelar was seized the same day. Camarena was interrogated and tortured for roughly 30 hours, the sessions recorded on tape and his captors injecting stimulants to keep him conscious, and he died on 9 February; the autopsy found his skull had been perforated with a drill. The bodies were found on 5 March 1985 on a ranch near La Angostura, Michoacán. Washington's response — border shutdowns, the drug-certification regime, and Operation Leyenda, the largest homicide investigation in DEA history — broke the Guadalajara Cartel.","notable":["Joined the DEA in 1974 and was posted to Guadalajara in 1980","His intelligence led to the November 1984 destruction of the Rancho Búfalo plantation in Allende, Chihuahua","Abducted 7 February 1985 in Guadalajara and murdered on 9 February after some 30 hours of torture","Body recovered 5 March 1985 near La Angostura, Michoacán, alongside that of pilot Alfredo Zavala Avelar","His murder triggered Operation Leyenda, the US drug-certification law, and Red Ribbon Week"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Camarena_(DEA_agent)","https://www.dea.gov/history","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guadalajara_Cartel"],"verified":true},{"id":"juan_ramon_matta_ballesteros","name":"Juan Ramón Matta-Ballesteros","alias":"El Negro","cartels":["guadalajara","other"],"born":1945,"died":2025,"role":"Honduran broker who linked the Medellín Cartel to the Guadalajara Cartel and pioneered the Central American cocaine corridor","status_2026":"Died 30 October 2025, aged 80, at the US Medical Center for Federal Prisoners in Springfield, Missouri, after 37 years in US custody","bio":"Born 12 January 1945 in Tegucigalpa, Honduras, Matta-Ballesteros became the essential middleman of the 1980s cocaine trade, connecting Pablo Escobar's Medellín Cartel with Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo's Guadalajara organization. He built airstrips and transport networks across Honduras and owned SETCO, an airline that US congressional investigators later found had been used to fly supplies to the Nicaraguan Contras while also moving cocaine — the basis of enduring allegations about US tolerance of his operations. Convicted in 1970 of entering the United States on a false passport, he escaped from the federal prison camp at Eglin, Florida, the following year. Indicted again in 1985 over major cocaine smuggling, he was detained in Colombia and escaped that country's custody in March 1986, allegedly by bribery, returning to Honduras, where he was legally untouchable because the constitution barred extradition of nationals. In April 1988 Honduran forces seized him and put him on a plane to the Dominican Republic; US marshals took custody in Puerto Rico in what critics called an illegal rendition, and riots in Tegucigalpa that week burned part of the US embassy annex. He received life without parole on narcotics charges and was also convicted in Los Angeles over the Camarena kidnapping, though a judge vacated those counts in 2017 and prosecutors dropped them in December 2018. He died in US custody on 30 October 2025.","notable":["Linked the Medellín and Guadalajara cartels, opening the Central American cocaine corridor","Owned SETCO, an airline tied to both cocaine flights and Contra resupply operations","Escaped a Florida federal prison camp in 1971 and Colombian custody in March 1986","Seized in Honduras in April 1988 and delivered to US marshals via the Dominican Republic and Puerto Rico","Convicted in the Camarena case, but those counts were vacated in 2017 and dropped in 2018; his life drug sentence stood until his death in 2025"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Ram%C3%B3n_Matta-Ballesteros","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enrique_Camarena_(DEA_agent)","https://insightcrime.org/"],"verified":true},{"id":"pablo_acosta_villarreal","name":"Pablo Acosta Villarreal","alias":"El Zorro de Ojinaga","cartels":["juarez"],"born":1937,"died":1987,"role":"Boss of the Ojinaga plaza on the Chihuahua-Texas border and mentor to Amado Carrillo Fuentes","status_2026":"Killed 24 April 1987 in a Mexican federal police raid at Santa Elena, Chihuahua","bio":"Born 26 January 1937 in Manuel Benavides, Chihuahua, Pablo Acosta Villarreal grew up between the Big Bend borderlands and West Texas, where he served time in a US prison before returning to Mexico. By the late 1970s he had made the dusty river town of Ojinaga his fiefdom, controlling smuggling along roughly 200 miles of the Chihuahua-Texas frontier with the tolerance of federal police commanders and the Dirección Federal de Seguridad. At his height between 1984 and 1986 he moved an estimated 60 tons of cocaine a year for Colombian suppliers, alongside marijuana and heroin, and Ojinaga became the first true Mexican cocaine transshipment hub. Among the young operators who learned the trade under him was Amado Carrillo Fuentes, whom Acosta took on as protégé and partner and who would later inherit and vastly expand the network as the Juárez Cartel. Acosta gave a remarkable series of interviews to American journalist Terrence Poppa, describing his business and his protectors — publicity that made him expendable. On 24 April 1987 Mexican federal police helicopters, with FBI logistical support, raided the tiny Rio Grande hamlet of Santa Elena, Chihuahua; Acosta died in the ensuing firefight, aged 50.","notable":["Controlled the Ojinaga plaza, the first major Mexican cocaine transshipment point on the Texas border","Moved an estimated 60 tons of cocaine a year at his peak in the mid-1980s","Mentored Amado Carrillo Fuentes, the future 'Lord of the Skies'","Gave candid interviews to reporter Terrence Poppa, later the book 'Drug Lord'","Killed 24 April 1987 in a federal police raid on Santa Elena, Chihuahua, backed by the FBI"],"state":"CHH","lat":29.5644,"lng":-104.4158,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pablo_Acosta_Villarreal","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ju%C3%A1rez_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rafael_Aguilar_Guajardo"],"verified":true},{"id":"juan_nepomuceno_guerra","name":"Juan Nepomuceno Guerra","alias":"Don Juan","cartels":["gulf"],"born":1915,"died":2001,"role":"Matamoros smuggling patriarch who founded the organization that became the Gulf Cartel","status_2026":"Died 12 July 2001 of respiratory disease in Matamoros, having spent almost no time in prison","bio":"Born 18 July 1915 in Matamoros, Tamaulipas, Juan Nepomuceno Guerra began smuggling whisky across the Rio Grande into south Texas during the 1930s, in the last years of the American Prohibition economy. Over the following decades he built a contraband empire in the lower Rio Grande valley — liquor, cigarettes, stolen cars, guns and eventually heroin and marijuana — sustained less by violence than by a dense web of political protection inside the ruling PRI, local law enforcement and Tamaulipas business. Operating from his Piedras Negras restaurant in Matamoros, where he held court openly for decades, he became known as the godfather of the border smuggling trade and the model for later cartel-state accommodation. In the 1970s and 1980s he brought his nephew Juan García Ábrego into the business; García Ábrego reoriented the organization toward cocaine, negotiating directly with the Cali Cartel and turning his uncle's contraband network into the modern Gulf Cartel. Guerra was briefly detained in 1991 in connection with a killing but was never seriously prosecuted, and by most accounts spent no more than a few hours in a cell across his entire career. He died in Matamoros on 12 July 2001, aged 85, of respiratory disease.","notable":["Began as a Prohibition-era whisky smuggler across the Rio Grande in the 1930s","Built the Matamoros contraband network that became the Gulf Cartel","Sustained by decades of PRI political protection rather than by violence","Brought his nephew Juan García Ábrego into the trade, who converted it to cocaine","Never meaningfully prosecuted; died of natural causes in Matamoros on 12 July 2001"],"state":"TAM","lat":25.8797,"lng":-97.5042,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Nepomuceno_Guerra","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulf_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Garc%C3%ADa_%C3%81brego"],"verified":true},{"id":"juan_garcia_abrego","name":"Juan García Ábrego","alias":"La Muñeca","cartels":["gulf"],"born":1944,"died":0,"role":"Gulf Cartel boss of the late 1980s and early 1990s; first trafficker placed on the FBI's Ten Most Wanted list","status_2026":"Alive, aged 81, and imprisoned in the United States since 1996 serving eleven consecutive life sentences; reported held at USP Hazelton, West Virginia","bio":"Born 13 September 1944 — US authorities placed his birth in La Paloma, Texas, making him an American citizen, while Mexican accounts give Matamoros, Tamaulipas — Juan García Ábrego learned smuggling from his uncle Juan Nepomuceno Guerra. In the 1980s he pushed the family's contraband network into cocaine, striking deals directly with Colombia's Cali Cartel in which the Gulf organization took a share of each shipment rather than a flat transport fee — a shift that made Mexican traffickers principals rather than couriers and multiplied their wealth. By the early 1990s he controlled the eastern corridor into Texas and had bought protection reaching into the federal judicial police and, US prosecutors alleged, the highest levels of the Salinas administration; his cartel was estimated to move a third of the cocaine entering the United States. In 1995 he became the first drug trafficker added to the FBI's Ten Most Wanted Fugitives list. Mexican federal agents captured him on a ranch outside Monterrey, Nuevo León, on 14 January 1996 and, because he was deemed a US citizen by birth, he was flown to Houston within hours rather than going through extradition. A Houston jury convicted him in 1996 on 22 counts of drug trafficking, money laundering and running a continuing criminal enterprise; Judge Ewing Werlein imposed eleven consecutive life terms and a $350 million forfeiture.","notable":["Restructured Gulf Cartel deals with the Cali Cartel to take payment in cocaine, not cash","At his peak reputedly moved about a third of US-bound cocaine","First drug trafficker named to the FBI's Ten Most Wanted Fugitives list, in 1995","Captured near Monterrey on 14 January 1996 and flown to Houston the same day as a US citizen","Convicted on 22 counts, sentenced to eleven consecutive life terms and $350 million forfeiture; still at USP Hazelton in 2026"],"state":"TAM","lat":25.8797,"lng":-97.5042,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Garc%C3%ADa_%C3%81brego","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulf_Cartel","https://www.justice.gov/"],"verified":true},{"id":"rafael_aguilar_guajardo","name":"Rafael Aguilar Guajardo","alias":"El Comandante","cartels":["juarez","guadalajara"],"born":1950,"died":1993,"role":"DFS commander turned Juárez Cartel co-founder who led the organization from 1987 to 1993","status_2026":"Assassinated 12 April 1993 in Cancún, Quintana Roo, in a killing attributed to Amado Carrillo Fuentes","bio":"Born in 1950 in Ciudad Juárez, Chihuahua, Rafael Aguilar Guajardo was a commander in the Dirección Federal de Seguridad, the Mexican interior ministry's political police, and embodied the fusion of state security apparatus and drug trafficking that defined the 1980s. Using DFS credentials and protection, he operated inside the Guadalajara Cartel's orbit while building his own interests along the Chihuahua border. When Pablo Acosta Villarreal was killed in April 1987 and the DFS was being dismantled in the fallout from the Camarena murder, Aguilar Guajardo took over the Ciudad Juárez corridor, leading the organization that would be called the Juárez Cartel and which he is credited with co-founding. He promoted the young Amado Carrillo Fuentes, a nephew of Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo, to be his second-in-command and operational chief, an arrangement that lasted until Carrillo Fuentes decided he no longer needed a patron. On 12 April 1993, while on holiday with his family in Cancún, Quintana Roo, Aguilar Guajardo was shot dead outside the Gypsys restaurant on the resort strip as he returned from a submarine excursion; family members and a bystander were also hit. Reporting attributes the killing to Carrillo Fuentes, allegedly ordered two days after Aguilar threatened to expose his high-level government contacts. Carrillo Fuentes then took sole control of the Juárez organization.","notable":["Served as a commander of the Dirección Federal de Seguridad while trafficking drugs","Took over the Ciudad Juárez plaza after Pablo Acosta's death in April 1987","Co-founder of the Juárez Cartel and its leader 1987-1993, with Amado Carrillo Fuentes as his deputy","Shot dead outside a Cancún restaurant on 12 April 1993 while vacationing with his family","His killing, attributed to Carrillo Fuentes, handed the Juárez Cartel to the 'Lord of the Skies'"],"state":"CHH","lat":31.6904,"lng":-106.4245,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rafael_Aguilar_Guajardo","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ju%C3%A1rez_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amado_Carrillo_Fuentes"],"verified":true},{"id":"amado_carrillo_fuentes","name":"Amado Carrillo Fuentes","alias":"El Señor de los Cielos","cartels":["juarez","guadalajara"],"born":1954,"died":1997,"role":"Juárez Cartel boss who industrialised cocaine transport by air and became the richest trafficker of his era","status_2026":"Died July 1997 in Mexico City after cosmetic surgery intended to disguise his appearance","bio":"Born 17 December 1954 in Guamuchilito, Navolato, Sinaloa, Amado Carrillo Fuentes entered the trade under his uncle Ernesto Fonseca Carrillo in the Guadalajara Cartel and learned border operations in Ojinaga from Pablo Acosta Villarreal and Rafael Aguilar Guajardo. After Aguilar Guajardo's assassination in Cancún in April 1993 — which reporting attributes to Carrillo Fuentes — he took undisputed control of the Juárez Cartel. His innovation was scale and altitude: he assembled a fleet of commercial-sized jets, including Boeing 727s, to fly multi-ton cocaine loads from Colombia into northern Mexico, earning the name 'Lord of the Skies' and, by US estimates, a fortune of around 25 billion dollars. He also perfected the purchase of the state. In February 1997 Mexico's newly appointed anti-drug czar, General Jesús Gutiérrez Rebollo, was revealed to have taken his money, a scandal that broke days before a US certification vote. With pressure mounting, Carrillo Fuentes sought to change his face and fingerprints, undergoing extensive plastic surgery and liposuction at the Santa Mónica hospital in Mexico City. He died there on 4 July 1997 (some accounts give 5 July), aged 42, of complications reported variously as an anaesthetic reaction or respirator failure. On 7 November 1997 two of his surgeons were found tortured and encased in concrete inside steel drums.","notable":["Ran a fleet of jetliners, including Boeing 727s, to fly multi-ton cocaine loads from Colombia","Took over the Juárez Cartel after the 1993 killing of Rafael Aguilar Guajardo","Corrupted General Jesús Gutiérrez Rebollo, Mexico's anti-drug czar, exposed in February 1997","Estimated by US authorities to have amassed a fortune of some 25 billion dollars","Died 4 July 1997 in Mexico City during plastic surgery; his surgeons were found murdered that November"],"state":"CHH","lat":31.6904,"lng":-106.4245,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amado_Carrillo_Fuentes","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ju%C3%A1rez_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jes%C3%BAs_Guti%C3%A9rrez_Rebollo"],"verified":true},{"id":"vicente_carrillo_fuentes","name":"Vicente Carrillo Fuentes","alias":"El Viceroy","cartels":["juarez"],"born":1962,"died":0,"role":"Juárez Cartel boss after his brother Amado's death; commander in the Juárez-Sinaloa war of 2008-2012","status_2026":"In US federal custody after his transfer from Mexico on 27 February 2025; arraigned in Brooklyn on 28 February 2025, where he pleaded not guilty, and facing charges brought in the Western District of Texas. Reports of plea negotiations and a late-2026 hearing could not be independently confirmed here.","bio":"Born 16 October 1962 in Navolato, Sinaloa, Vicente Carrillo Fuentes inherited the Juárez Cartel after his brother Amado died on the operating table in July 1997. Less charismatic and more brutal than his brother, he held the Ciudad Juárez plaza through the late 1990s and 2000s with the enforcement wing La Línea, staffed heavily by serving and former Chihuahua police. His alliance with Joaquín Guzmán collapsed after the 2004 killing of his brother Rodolfo Carrillo Fuentes and his wife outside a Culiacán cinema, blamed on Guzmán's people, and the retaliatory murder of Guzmán's brother Arturo in prison. The rupture became the Juárez-Sinaloa war that from 2008 turned Ciudad Juárez into the most violent city in the world, with more than 3,000 homicides in 2010 alone and atrocities including the January 2010 Villas de Salvárcar massacre of teenagers. The Sinaloa Cartel gradually won the corridor, and the Juárez organization fragmented. Mexican Army and federal police captured Carrillo Fuentes on 9 October 2014 in Torreón, Coahuila. A Mexican court sentenced him to 28 years on 14 September 2021, and on 27 February 2025 he was among 29 prisoners Mexico handed to the United States.","notable":["Took over the Juárez Cartel in 1997 after the death of his brother Amado","Commanded La Línea, the cartel's police-staffed enforcement wing","Fought the 2008-2012 war with the Sinaloa Cartel that made Ciudad Juárez the world's deadliest city","Captured 9 October 2014 in Torreón, Coahuila; sentenced in Mexico to 28 years on 14 September 2021","Transferred to the United States on 27 February 2025 alongside Rafael Caro Quintero"],"state":"CHH","lat":31.6904,"lng":-106.4245,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vicente_Carrillo_Fuentes","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ju%C3%A1rez_Cartel","https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny"],"verified":true},{"id":"benjamin_arellano_felix","name":"Benjamín Arellano Félix","alias":"El Min","cartels":["tijuana"],"born":1952,"died":0,"role":"Strategic head of the Tijuana Cartel (Arellano Félix Organization)","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the United States serving a 25-year sentence at USP Coleman I, Florida (BOP register 00678-748), with release scheduled for 28 April 2032","bio":"Born 12 March 1952 in Culiacán, Sinaloa, Benjamín Arellano Félix was the strategist of the eleven-sibling clan that inherited the Tijuana plaza after Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo's 1989 arrest. Where his brother Ramón supplied violence, Benjamín supplied organisation: he recruited the sons of Tijuana's business elite as gunmen — the narcojuniors — bought Baja California's police and judiciary, and built a taxation system over every load crossing at San Ysidro and Otay Mesa. His war with Joaquín Guzmán defined the 1990s. On 24 May 1993 a shootout at Guadalajara airport involving Tijuana gunmen left Cardinal Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo dead, a scandal that forced Guzmán's flight and arrest and made the Arellano Félix name notorious. The cartel also ordered the November 1997 attack that wounded Tijuana journalist Jesús Blancornelas and killed his bodyguard. Ramón was killed in Mazatlán in February 2002, and Benjamín was captured weeks later, on 9 March 2002, by the Mexican Army in Puebla. Mexico held him until his transfer to the United States on 29 April 2011; he pleaded guilty in San Diego on 4 January 2012 to racketeering and money-laundering conspiracy and was sentenced on 2 April 2012 to 25 years.","notable":["Ran the Tijuana Cartel's strategy and corruption networks from the late 1980s","Recruited the 'narcojuniors', gunmen drawn from wealthy Tijuana families","Cartel gunmen killed Cardinal Posadas Ocampo at Guadalajara airport on 24 May 1993","Ordered the 1997 attack that wounded editor Jesús Blancornelas of the weekly Zeta","Captured 9 March 2002 in Puebla; extradited 29 April 2011 and sentenced 2 April 2012 to 25 years"],"state":"BCN","lat":32.5149,"lng":-117.0382,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjam%C3%ADn_Arellano_F%C3%A9lix","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tijuana_Cartel","https://www.justice.gov/"],"verified":true},{"id":"ramon_arellano_felix","name":"Ramón Arellano Félix","alias":"El Mon","cartels":["tijuana"],"born":1964,"died":2002,"role":"Chief enforcer of the Tijuana Cartel and its most feared killer","status_2026":"Killed 10 February 2002 in an exchange of fire with a municipal police officer in Mazatlán, Sinaloa","bio":"Born 31 August 1964 in Culiacán, Sinaloa, Ramón Arellano Félix was the violence at the centre of the Tijuana Cartel his brother Benjamín organised. Through the 1990s he personally led the organization's sicarios, cultivating both the narcojuniors of Tijuana's upper class and the San Diego street gang Logan Heights, whose members he used as cross-border gunmen. He drove the cartel's war against Joaquín Guzmán, including a 1992 machine-gun attack on Guzmán's entourage at the Christine discotheque in Puerto Vallarta. On 24 May 1993 Tijuana gunmen under his command opened fire at Guadalajara's airport; Cardinal Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo was killed in his car with fourteen shots, and the ensuing scandal made Ramón one of Mexico's most wanted men. He was indicted in the Southern District of California for conspiracy to import cocaine and marijuana, and on 18 September 1997 became the 451st fugitive added to the FBI's Ten Most Wanted list. On 10 February 2002 he was in Mazatlán, Sinaloa, reportedly hunting Ismael Zambada, when municipal police officer Ángel Antonio Arias Torres stopped him; Ramón shot the officer twice in the chest, and the dying officer returned fire, killing him with a single shot to the head. Zambada later testified that Guzmán had arranged the stop.","notable":["Led the Tijuana Cartel's enforcement wing and its war against Joaquín Guzmán","Used San Diego's Logan Heights gang as cross-border assassins","Commanded the gunmen at the 24 May 1993 Guadalajara airport shooting that killed Cardinal Posadas Ocampo","Added to the FBI's Ten Most Wanted Fugitives list on 18 September 1997 as its 451st fugitive","Killed 10 February 2002 in Mazatlán; the police officer who shot him died of his own wounds"],"state":"BCN","lat":32.5149,"lng":-117.0382,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ram%C3%B3n_Arellano_F%C3%A9lix","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tijuana_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Jes%C3%BAs_Posadas_Ocampo"],"verified":true},{"id":"enedina_arellano_felix","name":"Enedina Arellano Félix","alias":"La Jefa / La Narcomamá","cartels":["tijuana"],"born":1961,"died":0,"role":"Accountant and financial chief of the Tijuana Cartel who became its leader, one of few women to head a major cartel","status_2026":"At large, aged 65; designated a kingpin by the US Treasury since 2000 and named in US indictments; whereabouts undisclosed","bio":"Born 12 April 1961 in Mazatlán, Sinaloa, Enedina Arellano Félix graduated in accounting from a private university in Guadalajara and entered the family business through its books rather than its guns. Through the 1990s she managed the Tijuana Cartel's finances, and after the departure of its money manager in 2000 she took direct charge of laundering, moving proceeds through Baja California real estate, currency exchanges and front companies while maintaining supplier relationships in Colombia. The US Treasury designated her under the Foreign Narcotics Kingpin Designation Act in June 2000. The collapse of the male leadership pushed her to the front: Ramón was killed in February 2002, Benjamín captured in March 2002, Francisco Javier 'El Tigrillo' captured at sea in August 2006, and Eduardo 'El Doctor' arrested in October 2008. From then she led the organization jointly with her nephew Luis Fernando Sánchez Arellano, 'El Ingeniero' — the son of her sister Alicia, though he is frequently and wrongly described as Enedina's own son — steering it away from spectacular violence toward a quieter tax-and-service model in which the surviving Tijuana network charged fees to Sinaloa Cartel traffickers using the plaza. His arrest on 23 June 2014 in Tijuana left her the family's senior surviving figure. Unlike her brothers she has never been captured.","notable":["Trained accountant who ran the Tijuana Cartel's money laundering from the 1990s","Designated by the US Treasury under the Kingpin Act in June 2000","Took over the cartel after her brothers were killed or captured between 2002 and 2008","Led jointly with her nephew Luis Fernando Sánchez Arellano until his arrest on 23 June 2014","Shifted the organization to charging fees to rival traffickers rather than fighting for routes"],"state":"BCN","lat":32.5149,"lng":-117.0382,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enedina_Arellano_F%C3%A9lix","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luis_Fernando_S%C3%A1nchez_Arellano","https://home.treasury.gov/policy-issues/financial-sanctions/sanctions-programs-and-country-information/counter-narcotics-trafficking-sanctions"],"verified":true},{"id":"hector_palma_salazar","name":"Héctor Luis Palma Salazar","alias":"El Güero Palma","cartels":["sinaloa","guadalajara"],"born":1960,"died":0,"role":"Co-founder of the Sinaloa Cartel and partner of Joaquín Guzmán in the 1990s war with the Tijuana Cartel","status_2026":"Imprisoned in Mexico at the Altiplano maximum-security prison. A tribunal absolved him of organized-crime charges in 2023, but he remains held on a pending prosecution for the 1995 murder of two Nayarit police officers. Reports of an April 2026 court order for emergency medical care are unconfirmed here.","bio":"Born 29 April 1960 in Mocorito, Sinaloa, Héctor Luis Palma Salazar began as a car thief and gunman before rising under Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo. After the 1989 arrest of 'El Padrino' he took the Sinaloa Pacific routes in partnership with Joaquín Guzmán, Ismael Zambada and Juan José Esparragoza, forming the nucleus of the Sinaloa Cartel. His life turned on an act of revenge, when a Venezuelan associate working for the Arellano Félix brothers, Rafael Enrique Clavel, seduced Palma's wife, forced her to withdraw millions from his accounts, decapitated her and sent the head to Palma; the couple's two young children, Jesús and Nataly, were taken to Venezuela and thrown from a bridge near San Cristóbal. The atrocity helped ignite the Sinaloa-Tijuana war of the 1990s. Palma survived a 1993 assassination attempt and continued running loads until 23 June 1995, when the jet carrying him crash-landed near Tepic, Nayarit, and federal police found him being sheltered by a group of state police commanders; 33 officers were arrested with him. Convicted in Mexico, he was extradited to the United States in 2007, served a cocaine-trafficking sentence at Atwater, California, and was returned to Mexico in June 2016, where he was immediately re-arrested on outstanding charges.","notable":["Co-founded the Sinaloa Cartel with Guzmán, Zambada and Esparragoza after 1989","His wife was beheaded and his two children thrown from a bridge in Venezuela by a rival working for the Arellano Félix brothers","Captured 23 June 1995 near Tepic after a plane crash, along with 33 protecting police officers","Extradited to the US in 2007, returned to Mexico in June 2016 and immediately re-arrested","Acquitted of organized-crime charges in 2023 but still held at Altiplano over a 1995 double police killing"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7994,"lng":-107.3897,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%A9ctor_Luis_Palma_Salazar","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinaloa_Cartel","https://www.bbc.com/mundo"],"verified":true},{"id":"joaquin_guzman_loera","name":"Joaquín Guzmán Loera","alias":"El Chapo","cartels":["sinaloa","chapitos","guadalajara"],"born":1957,"died":0,"role":"Sinaloa Cartel co-founder and its most famous boss; twice escaped Mexican maximum-security prisons","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the United States for life without parole plus 30 years at ADX Florence, Colorado (BOP register 89914-053). Reports of a 2026 ruling denying his transfer to Mexico are unconfirmed here.","bio":"Born 4 April 1957 in La Tuna, Badiraguato, Sinaloa, Joaquín Guzmán Loera worked opium fields as a boy and entered trafficking under Miguel Ángel Félix Gallardo, running logistics in the 1980s Guadalajara Cartel. After the 1989 arrest of 'El Padrino' he took the Sinaloa routes with Héctor Palma, Ismael Zambada and Juan José Esparragoza, pioneering cross-border tunnels beneath Agua Prieta and Douglas and later refrigerated-truck and rail concealment. His war with the Arellano Félix brothers produced the 24 May 1993 Guadalajara airport shooting that killed Cardinal Posadas Ocampo; Guzmán fled and was arrested by the Guatemalan Army near Tapachula on 9 June 1993. He escaped from Puente Grande prison on 19 January 2001 — the official account had him hidden in a laundry cart, though investigators later established that a bribed guard opened his cell — and spent thirteen years as the world's most wanted trafficker while the Sinaloa Cartel expanded into Ciudad Juárez, Tijuana and beyond. Recaptured in Mazatlán on 22 February 2014, he escaped again on 11 July 2015 through a mile-long tunnel dug into his cell at Altiplano, was retaken in Los Mochis in January 2016 and extradited on 19 January 2017. A Brooklyn jury convicted him on all counts on 12 February 2019; he was sentenced in July 2019 to life plus 30 years and a 12.6 billion dollar forfeiture.","notable":["Pioneered cross-border smuggling tunnels beneath the Arizona-Sonora line in the 1990s","Arrested by the Guatemalan Army on 9 June 1993 after the Posadas Ocampo killing","Escaped Puente Grande on 19 January 2001 and Altiplano on 11 July 2015 through a mile-long tunnel","Extradited to the US on 19 January 2017 and convicted in Brooklyn on 12 February 2019","Serving life plus 30 years at ADX Florence with a $12.6 billion forfeiture order"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7994,"lng":-107.3897,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joaqu%C3%ADn_%22El_Chapo%22_Guzm%C3%A1n","https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinaloa_Cartel"],"verified":true},{"id":"ismael_zambada_garcia","name":"Ismael Zambada García","alias":"El Mayo","cartels":["sinaloa","mayiza"],"born":1950,"died":0,"role":"Sinaloa Cartel co-founder and its longest-surviving leader, never imprisoned in Mexico across five decades","status_2026":"Sentenced on 20 July 2026 in Brooklyn federal court to life imprisonment; in US federal custody. Reporting of a roughly $15 billion forfeiture and of his request to serve the term in a prison medical facility could not be independently confirmed here.","bio":"Born 31 January 1950 in El Álamo, Culiacán, Sinaloa, Ismael Zambada García began as a farmhand and gunman and rose through the Guadalajara Cartel to become, after 1989, the quiet counterweight to Joaquín Guzmán within the Sinaloa organization. Where Guzmán courted celebrity, Zambada courted officials: US prosecutors alleged that he sustained four decades of trafficking by paying police, soldiers and politicians at every level. He built businesses in dairy, agriculture and transport around Culiacán, ran cocaine through Central America and the Pacific, and outlasted every rival, never once being arrested in Mexico. That ended on 25 July 2024, when he was flown to a private airfield near El Paso, Texas, and taken into US custody. Zambada says he was ambushed, bound and abducted by Joaquín Guzmán López, a son of El Chapo, under the pretext of a property meeting — an act that split the Sinaloa Cartel into the warring Chapitos and Mayiza factions and plunged Sinaloa into the bloodiest fighting in its history. He pleaded guilty on 25 August 2025 in Brooklyn to one count of racketeering conspiracy and one count of running a continuing criminal enterprise, and was sentenced to life imprisonment on 20 July 2026.","notable":["Co-founded the Sinaloa Cartel after 1989 and led it for 35 years without ever being arrested in Mexico","Prosecutors alleged systematic bribery of police, military and political officials","Taken into US custody on 25 July 2024 near El Paso; says he was abducted by Joaquín Guzmán López","His capture split the cartel into Chapitos and Mayiza factions, igniting the Sinaloa war of 2024-2026","Pleaded guilty 25 August 2025 and was sentenced to life imprisonment on 20 July 2026"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7994,"lng":-107.3897,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ismael_Zambada_Garc%C3%ADa","https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny","https://www.dea.gov/press-releases","https://insightcrime.org/"],"verified":true},{"id":"juan_jose_esparragoza_moreno","name":"Juan José Esparragoza Moreno","alias":"El Azul","cartels":["sinaloa","guadalajara","juarez"],"born":1949,"died":0,"role":"Sinaloa Cartel co-founder and the trade's chief mediator, a former federal security agent","status_2026":"Unresolved: reported dead on 7 June 2014, but no body was produced or forensically identified, and US authorities have continued to list him as a possible living fugitive","bio":"Born 3 February 1949 in Huixiopa, Badiraguato, Sinaloa (US databases list an alternative date of 2 March 1949), Juan José Esparragoza Moreno joined the Dirección Federal de Seguridad in the 1970s before moving fully into trafficking, a background that gave him unusual access to the Mexican state and to corrupt police commanders. Nicknamed 'El Azul' for his dark complexion, he was a member of the Guadalajara Cartel under Félix Gallardo and was imprisoned in the 1980s. After his release he became the least visible and most valuable figure of the Sinaloa Cartel's founding generation: a negotiator rather than a warlord, trusted enough to broker truces between the Sinaloa, Juárez and Beltrán Leyva organizations and to keep lines open to the Arellano Félix brothers. Both Mexican and US officials described him as the diplomat who held the federation together, and the US State Department's Narcotics Rewards Program offered five million dollars for him. On 7 June 2014 Mexican media reported that he had died of a heart attack at 65, some fifteen days after a car accident — accounts differ on whether this happened in Mexico City or Guadalajara — and that his remains had been cremated and sent to Culiacán. No body was recovered and no forensic confirmation was made public.","notable":["Former Dirección Federal de Seguridad agent who became a founding Sinaloa Cartel leader","Known as the trade's mediator, brokering truces between Sinaloa, Juárez and the Beltrán Leyva","Carried a five-million-dollar US State Department reward","Reported dead on 7 June 2014 after a car accident, with no body or forensic confirmation","Still carried by US authorities as possibly alive; his fate remains officially unresolved in 2026"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.7994,"lng":-107.3897,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Jos%C3%A9_Esparragoza_Moreno","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinaloa_Cartel","https://www.state.gov/narcotics-rewards-program/"],"verified":true},{"id":"ignacio_coronel_villarreal","name":"Ignacio Coronel Villarreal","alias":"Nacho Coronel / El Rey del Cristal","cartels":["sinaloa"],"born":1954,"died":2010,"role":"Sinaloa Cartel leader in Jalisco who industrialised methamphetamine production","status_2026":"Killed 29 July 2010 in a shootout with the Mexican Army in Zapopan, Jalisco","bio":"Born 1 February 1954 in Canelas, Durango, Ignacio Coronel Villarreal became one of the senior partners of the Sinaloa Cartel and its master of synthetic drugs. He began as a leader in Nayarit in the 1980s and later operated from Guadalajara and Zapopan, Jalisco, building the precursor-import and superlab network that turned Mexican crystal methamphetamine into a mass export product, taking over much of the business pioneered by the Amezcua Contreras brothers of Colima and earning the name 'King of Crystal'. He controlled Jalisco, Colima and Nayarit for the federation, oversaw cocaine reception on the Pacific coast, extended distribution into Texas and Arizona, and sat alongside Guzmán, Zambada and Esparragoza in the cartel's leadership. The US Treasury designated him under the Kingpin Act, he was indicted in the United States, and Washington offered five million dollars for information leading to his capture. On 29 July 2010 Mexican Army special forces raided a house in the Colinas de San Javier neighbourhood of Zapopan; Coronel opened fire, killing one soldier and wounding another, and was shot dead. His death did not end his network: his Jalisco lieutenants, calling themselves Los Torcidos and then the Mata Zetas, regrouped under Nemesio Oseguera Cervantes and Erick Valencia Salazar and by 2011 had constituted the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación.","notable":["Known as 'El Rey del Cristal' for industrialising Mexican methamphetamine production","Controlled Jalisco, Colima and Nayarit for the Sinaloa Cartel","Sat in the federation's top leadership with Guzmán, Zambada and Esparragoza","Killed 29 July 2010 in an army raid in Zapopan, Jalisco, after killing one soldier and wounding another","His Jalisco remnants regrouped in 2011 as the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.7214,"lng":-103.3918,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ignacio_Coronel_Villarreal","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jalisco_New_Generation_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sinaloa_Cartel"],"verified":true},{"id":"miguel_caro_quintero","name":"Miguel Ángel Caro Quintero","alias":"El Doctor","cartels":["sonora","guadalajara"],"born":1963,"died":0,"role":"Head of the Sonora Cartel, running the Caro Quintero family's marijuana and cocaine trade after his brother Rafael's imprisonment","status_2026":"Free in Mexico; completed a 17-year US sentence and was deported through the El Chaparral crossing at Tijuana on 20 July 2019. Mexican authorities issued no statement on his status and his current whereabouts are not publicly documented.","bio":"Born in 1963 in Caborca, Sonora, into a Sinaloan trafficking family originally from La Noria, Badiraguato, Miguel Ángel Caro Quintero took over the family's interests after his elder brother Rafael was arrested in Costa Rica in April 1985. From Agua Prieta and Caborca he ran the Sonora Cartel through the late 1980s and 1990s, moving marijuana and Colombian cocaine into Arizona through desert corridors and using family members, including his sister Sandra, to launder proceeds through ranches, cattle and legitimate businesses. Indicted in Colorado and Arizona and named a Kingpin Act target, he was one of the most-wanted traffickers of the decade. Mexican federal agents captured him in December 2001 in Los Mochis, Sinaloa. After years in Mexican custody he was extradited to the United States on 25 February 2009, pleaded guilty, and on 4 February 2010 was sentenced in Denver to 17 years for marijuana distribution conspiracy and racketeering. He shortened his time through education and rehabilitation programmes and was released and deported to Mexico on 20 July 2019. He has kept a low profile since. His brother Rafael's later Caborca Cartel operated in the same territory, but Miguel has not been publicly linked to its leadership.","notable":["Led the Sonora Cartel after his brother Rafael's 1985 arrest, smuggling into Arizona","Used family members, including his sister Sandra, for laundering through ranches and cattle","Captured in December 2001 in Los Mochis, Sinaloa","Extradited to the US on 25 February 2009 and sentenced in Denver on 4 February 2010 to 17 years","Released and deported to Mexico on 20 July 2019; free and living privately in 2026"],"state":"SON","lat":30.7167,"lng":-112.1594,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miguel_Caro_Quintero","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonora_Cartel","https://www.justice.gov/"],"verified":true},{"id":"amezcua_contreras_brothers","name":"Amezcua Contreras brothers (José de Jesús, Adán and Luis Ignacio)","alias":"Los Reyes de las Metanfetaminas","cartels":["colima"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Founders of the Colima Cartel, the first organization to industrialise Mexican methamphetamine production for export","status_2026":"Organization defunct as an independent structure, its remnants absorbed into larger networks in the Sinaloa orbit; the brothers served Mexican sentences and US extradition was blocked in 2002. Their individual whereabouts are not publicly documented.","bio":"Born in Colima in the late 1950s and early 1960s to a modest family, José de Jesús, Adán and Luis Ignacio Amezcua Contreras founded the Colima Cartel in 1988 under José de Jesús's leadership, operating principally from Guadalajara. Their innovation was structural: rather than transporting Colombian cocaine for a fee, they controlled a drug from raw material to street, importing ephedrine and pseudoephedrine in bulk from suppliers in Thailand, India and the Czech Republic and cooking methamphetamine in Mexican laboratories for sale in the western United States. By the mid-1990s the DEA described them as the world's largest methamphetamine traffickers and dubbed them the Kings of Meth, a model that every later Mexican organization, from Nacho Coronel's Jalisco network to the CJNG, would copy and scale. Adán was arrested on 10 November 1997 in Colima on weapons charges and again in May 2001 for money laundering. José de Jesús and Luis Ignacio were seized in Guadalajara on 1 June 1998; Luis Ignacio was sentenced to 49 years and sent to the Almoloya maximum-security prison. In May 2002 a Mexican federal court blocked José de Jesús's extradition to the United States, holding that the request did not satisfy Mexico's bar on extraditing people who could face life imprisonment. By 2008 the organization had no single leader, surviving only as precursor-diversion cells inside larger cartels.","notable":["Founded the Colima Cartel in 1988, the first Mexican meth-manufacturing organization","Imported ephedrine in bulk from Thailand, India and Europe rather than buying from Colombians","Called the 'Kings of Meth' by the DEA and reckoned the world's largest meth traffickers in the 1990s","Adán arrested November 1997; José de Jesús and Luis Ignacio arrested 1 June 1998 in Guadalajara, Luis Ignacio sentenced to 49 years","A Mexican court blocked José de Jesús's extradition to the US in May 2002 over the life-sentence bar"],"state":"COL","lat":19.2433,"lng":-103.7247,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colima_Cartel","https://www.dea.gov/press-releases","https://insightcrime.org/"],"verified":true},{"id":"pedro_diaz_parada","name":"Pedro Díaz Parada","alias":"El Cacique de Oaxaca","cartels":["oaxaca"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Founder and boss of the Oaxaca (Isthmus) cartel, a marijuana empire in the Isthmus of Tehuantepec","status_2026":"Uncertain. Reported to have been moved to home confinement on health grounds around 2019 and released from the Altiplano prison in October 2024, but this could not be verified against a primary or major-outlet source; treat his current status as unconfirmed.","bio":"Born in Santa María Zoquitlán, Oaxaca, in the 1940s, Pedro Díaz Parada built the country's most durable regional marijuana organization in the Isthmus of Tehuantepec, running plantations across the Oaxacan sierra and moving product north with his brothers through a family structure that dominated the state's Pacific corridor for three decades. He also served as a corridor host for Colombian cocaine transiting from Central America. His notoriety rests on his escapes and on his defiance of the courts. First captured in 1985 and sentenced to 33 years, he reportedly told the judge who convicted him that he would go free and the judge would die; he escaped, and the judge was later murdered. He escaped again in 1990 and, after a 1992 recapture, once more, remaining at liberty for years while his organization grew. Mexican forces finally arrested him on 17 January 2007 in the Oaxacan sierra, and he was sent to the Altiplano maximum-security prison. His career, spanning the 1970s to 2007, was among the longest continuous runs in Mexican trafficking, and his organization did not survive his final imprisonment as an independent force; the Isthmus corridor later passed to Zetas and CJNG cells.","notable":["Built the dominant marijuana organization of the Isthmus of Tehuantepec in Oaxaca","Escaped Mexican custody at least twice, in the 1980s and in 1990","Reportedly threatened the judge who sentenced him to 33 years; the judge was later killed","Recaptured 17 January 2007 in the Oaxacan sierra and imprisoned at Altiplano","One of the longest continuous trafficking careers in Mexico, running from the 1970s to 2007"],"state":"OAX","lat":16.5586,"lng":-96.2933,"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/","https://www.milenio.com/","https://www.proceso.com.mx/"],"verified":true},{"id":"jesus_gutierrez_rebollo","name":"Jesús Gutiérrez Rebollo","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1934,"died":2013,"role":"Mexican Army general and national anti-drug czar exposed in 1997 as an employee of Amado Carrillo Fuentes","status_2026":"Died 19 December 2013, aged 79, of brain cancer at the Central Military Hospital in Mexico City while serving his sentence","bio":"Born 19 April 1934 in Jonacatepec, Morelos, Jesús Gutiérrez Rebollo rose through the Mexican Army to command the Fifth Military Region in Guadalajara, where he earned a reputation as an aggressive drug fighter by arresting figures tied to the Sinaloa and Tijuana organizations. In December 1996 President Ernesto Zedillo named him head of the Instituto Nacional para el Combate a las Drogas, making him Mexico's top counter-narcotics official; US drug czar Barry McCaffrey publicly praised him. Within weeks investigators noticed he had moved into a luxury apartment in Mexico City whose rent no public salary could cover. The property was tied to the Juárez Cartel, and recordings captured him discussing payments with Amado Carrillo Fuentes in exchange for ignoring the organization's operations and targeting its rivals. He was detained on 6 February 1997, and the arrest was made public on 18 February, days before the annual US drug-certification vote — the single most damaging corruption scandal of the era and a case still cited as the definitive example of cartel capture of the Mexican security state. He was first sentenced to 31 years, 10 months and 15 days for the misuse of army-restricted weapons, and in 2007 a federal court added 40 years and a fine of some 24.7 million pesos for bribery, obstruction and facilitating cocaine shipments.","notable":["Commanded the Fifth Military Region in Guadalajara before heading Mexico's anti-drug agency","Appointed drug czar in December 1996 and praised by US officials as incorruptible","Detained 6 February 1997 and publicly exposed on 18 February for taking Juárez Cartel money","Sentenced to 31 years and 10 months, with a further 40-year term and a large fine imposed in 2007","Died of brain cancer at the Central Military Hospital in Mexico City on 19 December 2013"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1332,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jes%C3%BAs_Guti%C3%A9rrez_Rebollo","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amado_Carrillo_Fuentes","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ju%C3%A1rez_Cartel"],"verified":true},{"id":"juan_jesus_posadas_ocampo","name":"Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1926,"died":1993,"role":"Cardinal and Archbishop of Guadalajara, shot dead at Guadalajara airport in 1993 in Mexico's most disputed assassination","status_2026":"Assassinated May 1993; no one has ever been convicted of his murder and the official crossfire finding remains contested","bio":"Born 10 November 1926 in Salvatierra, Guanajuato, Juan Jesús Posadas Ocampo was ordained in 1950, consecrated bishop in 1970, and served as Bishop of Tijuana and later of Cuernavaca before becoming the eighth Archbishop of Guadalajara in 1987. Pope John Paul II made him a cardinal on 28 June 1991. On 24 May 1993, as he waited in his car in the parking area of Guadalajara's Miguel Hidalgo International Airport to meet the papal nuncio, gunmen opened fire; Posadas Ocampo was hit fourteen times at close range and killed along with six other people, including his driver. The government of Carlos Salinas de Gortari concluded within days that he had been caught in crossfire between Tijuana Cartel gunmen and Joaquín Guzmán's people, and that the assassins had mistaken his black Grand Marquis for Guzmán's. The Catholic Church rejected that account almost immediately, noting the point-blank wounds, the cardinal's distinctive clerical dress and pectoral cross, and the unimpeded departure of the shooters on a commercial flight. A deputy attorney general later documented serious irregularities in the investigation, including destroyed evidence. Three Logan Heights gang members from San Diego pleaded guilty in the United States to weapons charges and received 18 to 22-year terms, but no one has ever been convicted of the murder itself.","notable":["Eighth Archbishop of Guadalajara from 1987 and cardinal from 28 June 1991","Shot fourteen times at Guadalajara airport on 24 May 1993; six others died with him","The Salinas government blamed crossfire and mistaken identity involving Joaquín Guzmán","The Church rejected that finding, citing point-blank wounds and his visible clerical dress","Three Logan Heights gunmen pleaded guilty to US weapons charges, but no one was ever convicted of the killing"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Jes%C3%BAs_Posadas_Ocampo","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tijuana_Cartel","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joaqu%C3%ADn_%22El_Chapo%22_Guzm%C3%A1n"],"verified":true},{"id":"carlos_salinas_de_gortari","name":"Carlos Salinas de Gortari","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1948,"died":0,"role":"President of Mexico 1988-1994, whose administration presided over the cartels' consolidation and faced enduring allegations of protection","status_2026":"Alive, aged 78; never charged with any drug-related offence; a private citizen dividing his time between Mexico and Europe and still a recurring figure in Mexican political controversy","bio":"Born 3 April 1948 in Mexico City, Carlos Salinas de Gortari was a Harvard-trained economist and budget secretary who won the disputed 1988 election and governed until 1994. His presidency privatised state industries, negotiated NAFTA and opened the border to unprecedented commercial traffic — the same corridors and volumes that made cartel logistics vastly harder to police. He is included in this atlas for context rather than as a trafficker: he has never been charged with a drug offence, and no court has established that he directed protection of any cartel. But his sexenio was the period in which the Guadalajara Cartel's successors consolidated into the Tijuana, Juárez, Gulf and Sinaloa organizations, and it produced the era's defining scandals — the May 1993 assassination of Cardinal Posadas Ocampo and the government's contested crossfire explanation, and the 1994 killings of PRI candidate Luis Donaldo Colosio and party secretary José Francisco Ruiz Massieu. His brother Raúl Salinas was arrested in February 1995 and convicted in 1999 of masterminding the Ruiz Massieu murder, a conviction overturned in June 2005; Swiss and US investigations meanwhile traced more than 90 million dollars he had moved abroad, and Swiss authorities concluded a large share was drug protection money. US prosecutors in the Juan García Ábrego case also alleged high-level protection. Salinas has consistently denied all wrongdoing.","notable":["President of Mexico from 1 December 1988 to 30 November 1994","Negotiated NAFTA, whose commercial traffic reshaped cross-border smuggling economics","His government produced the contested crossfire finding in the 1993 Posadas Ocampo killing","Brother Raúl Salinas convicted in 1999 over the Ruiz Massieu murder and freed when it was overturned in June 2005; Swiss authorities linked his offshore accounts to drug protection payments","Never charged with a drug offence; has denied all allegations and remains alive in 2026"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1332,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlos_Salinas_de_Gortari","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ra%C3%BAl_Salinas_de_Gortari","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Jes%C3%BAs_Posadas_Ocampo"],"verified":true},{"id":"nemesio_oseguera_cervantes","name":"Nemesio Rubén Oseguera Cervantes","alias":"El Mencho","cartels":["milenio","cjng"],"born":1966,"died":2026,"role":"Founder and supreme leader of the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación (CJNG), 2011-2026","status_2026":"Killed by Mexican military forces in Tapalpa, Jalisco, 22 February 2026","bio":"Born 17 July 1966 in Aguililla, Michoacán, into a rural avocado- and lime-growing family. He migrated illegally to California as a teenager, was arrested in San Francisco in 1986 and again in 1989 for narcotics sales, and after a 1992 Sacramento federal drug case served roughly five years before deportation. Back in Mexico he briefly served as a municipal police officer in Jalisco before joining the Milenio (Valencia) Cartel, cementing his position by marrying Rosalinda González Valencia, sister of financier Abigael 'El Cuini' González Valencia. When Milenio's leadership collapsed in 2009-2010, Oseguera led the splinter group 'Los Mata Zetas', which by 2011 had rebranded as the CJNG. Under him the CJNG grew from a Jalisco enforcement wing into Mexico's most geographically dispersed cartel, notorious for downing an army helicopter in Jalisco in May 2015, the June 2020 ambush of Mexico City police chief Omar García Harfuch, and industrial-scale methamphetamine and fentanyl trafficking. The US reward for him reached US$15 million by December 2024, and the State Department designated the CJNG a Foreign Terrorist Organization on 20 February 2025. After nearly two decades as a fugitive in the Michoacán-Jalisco sierra, he was fatally wounded on 22 February 2026 during a Mexican military operation in Tapalpa, Jalisco, carried out with US intelligence support. His death triggered nationwide narco-blockades and a violent CJNG succession struggle.","notable":["Deported ex-convict and former Jalisco municipal police officer who became Mexico's most-wanted capo","Built the CJNG from the wreckage of the Milenio Cartel between 2009 and 2011","US reward reached US$15 million; Mexico offered MXN 300 million","CJNG designated a Foreign Terrorist Organization by the US on 20 February 2025","Killed 22 February 2026 in a Mexican military operation in Tapalpa, Jalisco"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Mencho","https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2026/02/22/jalisco-cartel-drugs-mexico-mencho-new-generation/d7d4820a-1014-11f1-8e8d-fe91db44677b_story.html","https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/mexican-drug-lord-el-mencho-killed-military-operation-government-source-2026-02-22","https://www.state.gov/nemesio-ruben-oseguera-cervantes-el-mencho-2/"],"verified":false},{"id":"abigael_gonzalez_valencia","name":"Abigael González Valencia","alias":"El Cuini","cartels":["cjng","milenio"],"born":1972,"died":0,"role":"Head of Los Cuinis, the CJNG's financial and money-laundering arm","status_2026":"Extradited to the United States 12 August 2025; in federal custody and reportedly cooperating with US prosecutors","bio":"Born 18 October 1972 in Aguililla, Michoacán, one of a large family that rose through the Milenio Cartel alongside the Oseguera brothers. His sister Rosalinda married Nemesio 'El Mencho' Oseguera, binding the two clans. After Milenio fractured around 2010, González Valencia consolidated Los Cuinis, a family network that handled the CJNG's finances, laundering drug proceeds through real estate, restaurants, hotels, resorts and shell companies across Mexico, Latin America, Europe and Asia. US officials described Los Cuinis as one of the wealthiest criminal organisations in the hemisphere; the Treasury Department sanctioned him and the network under the Kingpin Act in 2015. He was captured on 28 February 2015 at a restaurant in Puerto Vallarta, Jalisco, in a joint federal police and national security operation, and then spent more than a decade in Mexican maximum-security prisons fighting extradition through dozens of amparo appeals. On 12 August 2025 the Sheinbaum government sent him to the United States as part of a transfer of 26 senior cartel operatives, the second such mass handover after the 29 sent on 27 February 2025. By October 2025 US and Mexican outlets reported that he had begun cooperating with American authorities, and in March 2026 El País reported that several CJNG heavyweights in US custody were negotiating deals in the wake of El Mencho's death.","notable":["Brother-in-law of El Mencho; the marriage alliance created the CJNG-Cuinis financial structure","Arrested 28 February 2015 in a Puerto Vallarta restaurant","Fought extradition for over ten years with repeated amparo appeals","Sent to the US on 12 August 2025 with 25 other cartel operatives","Reported since October 2025 to be cooperating with US prosecutors"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abigael_Gonz%C3%A1lez_Valencia","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c8xkxr1en3zo","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_in_Mexico"],"verified":false},{"id":"rosalinda_gonzalez_valencia","name":"Rosalinda González Valencia","alias":"La Jefa","cartels":["cjng"],"born":1963,"died":0,"role":"Financial operator of the CJNG and Los Cuinis; widow of El Mencho","status_2026":"Released from Mexican prison in February 2025; whereabouts unknown as of 2026","bio":"Born in 1963 in Aguililla, Michoacán, sister of Abigael 'El Cuini' González Valencia. She married Nemesio Oseguera Cervantes in 1996, an alliance that fused the González Valencia family's financial machinery with the emerging CJNG. Mexican prosecutors and the US Treasury identified her as the manager of the cartel's legitimate-business front: investigators alleged she controlled some 70 to 73 companies, including car washes, pharmacies, real estate firms and resorts, laundering roughly US$63 million in 2015-2016 alone. She was first arrested by the Mexican Navy in Zapopan, Jalisco, on 26 May 2018 on money-laundering charges but was freed on bail on 6 September 2018 after posting about 1.5 million pesos. Mexican forces recaptured her in Zapopan on 15 November 2021 for violating those bail conditions. In December 2023 a federal judge sentenced her to five years for failing to report car-wash transactions, and she was granted early release for good conduct on 27 February 2025 from a federal prison in Morelos. After her husband was killed on 22 February 2026 she disappeared from public view; InSight Crime reported in April 2026 that her current whereabouts are unknown, amid speculation about her influence over the CJNG succession won by her son Juan Carlos Valencia González, 'El Pelón'.","notable":["Married El Mencho in 1996, uniting the Oseguera and González Valencia clans","Accused of managing more than 70 CJNG front companies","Arrested May 2018, freed on bail, recaptured 15 November 2021","Sentenced to five years in December 2023; released early 27 February 2025","Vanished from public view after El Mencho's death in February 2026"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.7214,"lng":-103.3918,"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/rosalinda-gonzalez-valencia-alias-la-jefa/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosalinda_Gonz%C3%A1lez_Valencia","https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/mexican-authorities-arrest-wife-powerful-jalisco-cartel-leader-el-mencho-2021-11-16/"],"verified":false},{"id":"ruben_oseguera_gonzalez","name":"Rubén Oseguera González","alias":"El Menchito","cartels":["cjng"],"born":1990,"died":0,"role":"Former second-in-command of the CJNG; son of El Mencho","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the United States; sentenced 7 March 2025 to life plus 30 years","bio":"Born 14 February 1990 in San Francisco County, California, giving him US citizenship, and raised partly in Jalisco as the eldest son of Nemesio Oseguera Cervantes and Rosalinda González Valencia. By his early twenties he was the CJNG's operational number two, directing cocaine and methamphetamine shipments and commanding armed cells. Mexican authorities arrested him on 30 January 2014, released him on a technicality, and recaptured him on 23 June 2015 in Zapopan, Jalisco. After years of litigation he was extradited to the United States on 21 February 2020, arriving at Dulles International Airport to face charges in the District of Columbia. His trial in Washington in September 2024 featured testimony from former lieutenants describing his role in mass killings and in the acquisition of military-grade weapons; prosecutors said he was linked to roughly 100 murders. Jurors convicted him on 20 and 24 September 2024 of international drug trafficking conspiracy and of using firearms, including destructive devices, in furtherance of it. On 7 March 2025 a federal judge sentenced him to life imprisonment plus 30 consecutive years, the harshest sentence yet imposed on a CJNG figure and a marker of the Trump administration's escalated posture toward Mexican cartels.","notable":["US citizen by birth, born in San Francisco County, California","CJNG's second-in-command; arrested 2014, released, recaptured June 2015","Extradited to the United States on 21 February 2020","Convicted in Washington, D.C. on 20 and 24 September 2024","Sentenced 7 March 2025 to life plus 30 years"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rub%C3%A9n_Oseguera_Gonz%C3%A1lez","https://apnews.com/article/el-menchito-cartel-sentenced-jalisco-cjng","https://www.cbsnews.com/news/el-menchito-ruben-oseguera-gonzalez-sentenced-life-prison/"],"verified":false},{"id":"ovidio_guzman_lopez","name":"Ovidio Guzmán López","alias":"El Ratón","cartels":["sinaloa","chapitos"],"born":1990,"died":0,"role":"Los Chapitos leader; central figure of the 2019 and 2023 Culiacán battles","status_2026":"In US custody in Chicago; pleaded guilty 11 July 2025 and is cooperating, sentencing not yet held","bio":"Born 29 March 1990, son of Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán Loera and Griselda López Pérez. He rose within Los Chapitos in the late 2010s, overseeing methamphetamine and fentanyl laboratories in Sinaloa. On 17 October 2019 National Guard troops detained him at a house in Culiacán; the Sinaloa Cartel flooded the city with gunmen, attacked soldiers' families and paralysed the state, and President Andrés Manuel López Obrador ordered him released to avoid a massacre, an event known as the Culiacanazo. Mexican forces recaptured him on 5 January 2023 in the Jesús María district north of Culiacán after a second wave of cartel violence that left about 30 dead, including ten soldiers. He was extradited to the United States on 15 September 2023 to face charges in the Northern District of Illinois. On 11 July 2025 he pleaded guilty in the Dirksen Federal Building in Chicago to two counts of drug distribution and two counts of participating in a continuing criminal enterprise, under an agreement to cooperate with US prosecutors in exchange for avoiding a life term. Reporting in 2025 indicated that relatives had been relocated to the United States as part of the arrangement. As of September 2026 no sentencing date had been made public.","notable":["Trigger of the 17 October 2019 Culiacanazo, when AMLO ordered his release","Recaptured 5 January 2023; about 30 people died in the ensuing violence","Extradited to the United States on 15 September 2023","Pleaded guilty in Chicago on 11 July 2025 to four counts and agreed to cooperate","Awaiting sentencing as of September 2026"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.8091,"lng":-107.394,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ovidio_Guzm%C3%A1n_L%C3%B3pez","https://www.justice.gov/usao-ndil/pr/chapitos-charged-departments-latest-actions-disrupt-flow-illegal-fentanyl-and-other","https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/jul/11/ovidio-guzman-lopez-guilty-plea"],"verified":false},{"id":"ismael_zambada_sicairos","name":"Ismael Zambada Sicairos","alias":"Mayito Flaco","cartels":["sinaloa","mayiza"],"born":1982,"died":0,"role":"Leader of La Mayiza, the Zambada faction of the Sinaloa Cartel","status_2026":"Fugitive; commands La Mayiza and, as of September 2026, holds most of Sinaloa","bio":"Born around 1982 in or near Culiacán, Sinaloa, one of an estimated sixteen children of Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada García. Unlike his brothers Vicente Zambada Niebla (arrested 2009), Serafín Zambada Ortiz (2013) and Ismael Zambada Imperial (2014), he has never been captured. He was indicted in the United States in 2014 on drug-trafficking conspiracy charges, with the indictment unsealed in January 2015, and is accused of moving cocaine, methamphetamine, heroin, fentanyl and precursor chemicals through Central and South America and Asia. His deliberate low profile long spared him the scrutiny his brothers attracted. After his father was seized in El Paso on 25 July 2024, he assumed leadership of the Zambada wing, known as La Mayiza, and on 9 September 2024 launched coordinated attacks on Los Chapitos that opened the Sinaloa civil war. His forces operate in southern Culiacán, the Golden Triangle spanning Sinaloa, Durango and Chihuahua, and in Zacatecas, Sonora, Baja California and Colima. La Mayiza secured an alliance with Fausto Isidro 'Chapo Isidro' Meza Flores' Guasave organisation in May 2025 and with remnants of the old Zeta structure that October. The DEA issued a formal wanted listing for him in September 2025. By September 2026 InSight Crime assessed that he was still gaining territory and was positioned to head a reconfigured Sinaloa Cartel.","notable":["Son of El Mayo Zambada and the only major Zambada son never captured","Indicted in the US in 2014; indictment unsealed January 2015","Took over La Mayiza after his father's capture on 25 July 2024","Opened the Sinaloa civil war with coordinated attacks on 9 September 2024","DEA issued a wanted listing for him in September 2025; still at large in 2026"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.8091,"lng":-107.394,"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/ismael-zambada-sicairos-alias-mayito-flaco/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ismael_Zambada_Sicairos","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infighting_in_the_Sinaloa_Cartel"],"verified":false},{"id":"aureliano_guzman_loera","name":"Aureliano Guzmán Loera","alias":"El Guano","cartels":["sinaloa"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Head of the 'Gente del Guano' Sinaloa Cartel faction in the Golden Triangle; brother of El Chapo","status_2026":"Fugitive; US$5 million US reward; evaded a major Sinaloa operation in April 2026","bio":"Born in La Tuna, Badiraguato, Sinaloa, in the Golden Triangle highlands, Aureliano Guzmán Loera is a younger brother of Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán Loera; his birth year has never been publicly confirmed by US or Mexican authorities. He remained in the sierra of Badiraguato while his brother built a transnational empire, controlling opium poppy and marijuana cultivation, clandestine airstrips and trafficking corridors across the Sinaloa-Durango-Chihuahua border zone. His cell, widely known as the Gente del Guano, has fought long-running feuds with the Beltrán Leyva remnants and with local rivals in Tamazula, Durango. On 8 November 2021 the US State Department announced a US$5 million reward for information leading to his arrest, alongside rewards for El Chapo's sons and the Salgueiro brothers of Chihuahua, and he was indicted in US federal court on narcotics conspiracy charges. Analysts have described him as an 'old boss' figure, wealthy and locally powerful but without national reach. In the Sinaloa civil war that began in September 2024 his faction aligned with Los Chapitos against La Mayiza. Mexican forces mounted operations to capture him in December 2025 and again in April 2026, when troops backed by US intelligence detained about ten of his men in Sinaloa; he escaped both times and remained a fugitive as of September 2026.","notable":["Younger brother of El Chapo; based in La Tuna, Badiraguato","Controls opium and marijuana production in the Golden Triangle","US$5 million State Department reward announced 8 November 2021","Aligned with Los Chapitos against La Mayiza in the Sinaloa civil war","Escaped Mexican operations in December 2025 and April 2026"],"state":"SIN","lat":25.3608,"lng":-107.5525,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infighting_in_the_Sinaloa_Cartel","https://www.vice.com/en/article/the-us-just-put-a-5-million-bounty-on-el-chapos-brother/","https://english.elpais.com/international/2024-07-12/el-guano-guzman-and-the-old-boss-syndrome-of-the-sinaloa-cartel.html"],"verified":false},{"id":"damaso_lopez_nunez","name":"Dámaso López Núñez","alias":"El Licenciado","cartels":["sinaloa"],"born":1966,"died":0,"role":"Former Puente Grande prison official who became a senior Sinaloa Cartel boss and El Chapo's compadre","status_2026":"Imprisoned in the United States; pleaded guilty September 2018 and cooperated, sentence later reduced","bio":"Born 22 February 1966 in Eldorado, Sinaloa. He trained as a lawyer, worked for the Sinaloa state judicial police and then became deputy director of security at the Puente Grande federal prison in Jalisco, where Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán was held; he is widely credited with facilitating Guzmán's January 2001 escape, after which he resigned and joined the Sinaloa Cartel. Over the next fifteen years he became one of the organisation's most powerful operators, godfather to Guzmán's children and controller of trafficking corridors through Sinaloa and the port of Manzanillo. After El Chapo's 2016 recapture and January 2017 extradition, López Núñez and his son Dámaso López Serrano fought a bloody succession war against Iván Archivaldo and Jesús Alfredo Guzmán Salazar that convulsed Culiacán through 2016 and 2017. Mexican marines and federal investigators captured him on 2 May 2017 in the Anzures neighbourhood of Mexico City. He was extradited to the United States and pleaded guilty to cocaine trafficking in September 2018. In January 2019 he testified for the prosecution at El Chapo's trial in Brooklyn, describing the cartel's structure and its bribes to officials. His original life term was subsequently reduced in recognition of that cooperation, with Spanish-language reporting placing his projected release in 2032.","notable":["Deputy security director at Puente Grande prison during El Chapo's January 2001 escape","Godfather to El Chapo's children and one of the cartel's top operators","Fought Los Chapitos for the succession in 2016-2017","Arrested in Mexico City on 2 May 2017 and extradited to the US","Pleaded guilty September 2018 and testified against El Chapo in January 2019"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.8091,"lng":-107.394,"sources":["https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%A1maso_L%C3%B3pez_N%C3%BA%C3%B1ez","https://www.bbc.com/mundo/noticias-america-latina-39779090","https://cpj.org/data/people/javier-valdez-cardenas/"],"verified":false},{"id":"damaso_lopez_serrano","name":"Dámaso López Serrano","alias":"Mini Lic","cartels":["sinaloa"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Sinaloa Cartel heir turned US government witness; alleged instigator of Javier Valdez's murder","status_2026":"In US custody; sentenced in February 2026 to five years for attempted fentanyl trafficking after being re-arrested in December 2024","bio":"Son of Dámaso López Núñez, he grew up inside the Sinaloa Cartel's inner circle and became notorious for flaunting weapons, exotic cars and animals on social media. When his father was captured on 2 May 2017, he was left fighting Los Chapitos alone in Culiacán and Baja California Sur. Rather than face them he surrendered at the Calexico port of entry to US authorities on 27 July 2017, the first significant Mexican capo to hand himself in voluntarily, and was arraigned in San Diego in August 2017. He pleaded guilty to narcotics charges in January 2018 and, in exchange for extensive cooperation, received a sentence that allowed his release after a few years in custody; he remained in the United States under government protection. On 24 January 2020 Mexico's federal special prosecutor for crimes against freedom of expression, FEADLE, obtained an arrest warrant naming him as the intellectual author of the 15 May 2017 murder of Culiacán journalist Javier Valdez Cárdenas, allegedly ordered because Ríodoce published an interview with his father in February 2017; Mexico never secured his extradition. In December 2024 US authorities re-arrested him on charges of attempting to traffic fentanyl, with reporting alleging he had sought to rejoin the cartel. In February 2026 he was sentenced to five years in prison for that offence.","notable":["Surrendered voluntarily at the Calexico border crossing on 27 July 2017","Pleaded guilty in San Diego in January 2018 and became a cooperating witness","Named by Mexican prosecutors on 24 January 2020 as mastermind of Javier Valdez's murder","Re-arrested in the US in December 2024 for attempted fentanyl trafficking","Sentenced in February 2026 to five years"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.8091,"lng":-107.394,"sources":["https://cpj.org/data/people/javier-valdez-cardenas/","https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/el-mini-lic-re-arrested-in-us/","https://insightcrime.org/news/son-captured-sinaloa-cartel-leader-surrenders-us/"],"verified":false},{"id":"rodrigo_paez_quintero","name":"Rodrigo Páez Quintero","alias":"El R","cartels":["caborca"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Caborca Cartel cell commander; nephew of Rafael Caro Quintero","status_2026":"Extradited to the United States in July 2024; facing federal drug-trafficking charges in Arizona","bio":"A nephew of Rafael Caro Quintero, the Guadalajara Cartel co-founder who was released from a Mexican prison in 2013 and returned to trafficking in Sonora. When Caro Quintero rebuilt his organisation as the Caborca Cartel around 2019, Páez Quintero commanded one of its principal armed cells, known variously as Los Páez and Los Paleteros, operating in Sonora and along the Arizona border. Mexican naval intelligence warned in the early 2020s of an escalating internal conflict between his cell and rival Caborca factions, part of the violence that has scarred Caborca, Altar and Sonoyta as the organisation fought the Sinaloa Cartel for the Sásabe and Altar smuggling corridors. Mexican federal prosecutors and the navy arrested him on 30 April 2023 in the Guadalajara metropolitan area. He was indicted in Arizona on charges of conspiring to smuggle multi-kilogram quantities of methamphetamine, cocaine and heroin into Phoenix and Tucson, and on 15-17 July 2024 Mexico extradited him to the United States, where he was arraigned in the District of Arizona. His transfer preceded the far larger handovers of February and August 2025 and formed part of the pressure campaign that also produced Caro Quintero's own transfer to US custody on 27 February 2025.","notable":["Nephew of Rafael Caro Quintero and a Caborca Cartel cell commander","Led the armed cell known as Los Páez or Los Paleteros in Sonora","Arrested 30 April 2023 in the Guadalajara metropolitan area","Extradited to the United States in mid-July 2024 to face charges in Arizona","His case preceded Caro Quintero's own transfer to the US in February 2025"],"state":"SON","lat":30.7167,"lng":-112.1667,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caborca_Cartel","https://www.tucsonsentinel.com/local/report/071724_caro_quintero_nephew/","https://www.newsweek.com/mexican-kingpin-extradited-us-drug-trafficking-charges-1925487"],"verified":false},{"id":"hurtado_olascoaga_brothers","name":"Johnny and José Alfredo Hurtado Olascoaga","alias":"El Pez / El Fresa","cartels":["nueva_familia_michoacana"],"born":1984,"died":0,"role":"Brothers who jointly lead La Nueva Familia Michoacana across Guerrero, Michoacán and Estado de México","status_2026":"Both fugitives; indicted and sanctioned by the US in April 2025 with a combined US$8 million reward","bio":"José Alfredo Hurtado Olascoaga, 'El Fresa', was born on 2 September 1984 in Arcelia, Guerrero; his elder brother Johnny, 'El Pez', has no publicly confirmed birth year. They took joint command of La Nueva Familia Michoacana on 1 July 2014 after the arrest of José María Chávez Magaña, rebuilding a splinter of the original La Familia Michoacana into one of Mexico's most violent regional organisations. From the Tierra Caliente straddling Guerrero, Michoacán and Estado de México they run trafficking of cocaine, methamphetamine and fentanyl to the United States alongside poppy and marijuana cultivation, illegal iron-ore mining and systematic extortion, with a presence reported in at least 35 municipalities. Their forces were blamed for the ambush at Coatepec Harinas, Estado de México, on 18 March 2021 that killed thirteen police officers, and 'El Fresa' publicly claimed that the San Miguel Totolapan massacre of 5 October 2022, in which twenty people including the mayor were killed, had targeted him. The US Treasury sanctioned the organisation and its leaders under Executive Order 14059 in November 2022. The State Department designated La Nueva Familia Michoacana a Foreign Terrorist Organization on 20 February 2025, and on 16 April 2025 the Justice Department unsealed indictments against both brothers with an US$8 million combined reward, followed by further Treasury sanctions on 22 April 2025. Both remained fugitives in September 2026.","notable":["Took over La Nueva Familia Michoacana on 1 July 2014","Operate across the Tierra Caliente of Guerrero, Michoacán and Estado de México","Blamed for the Coatepec Harinas police ambush of 18 March 2021","Organisation designated a US Foreign Terrorist Organization on 20 February 2025","US indictments and an US$8 million reward announced 16 April 2025; both still at large"],"state":"GRO","lat":18.3167,"lng":-100.2833,"sources":["https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_Alfredo_Hurtado_Olascoaga","https://www.cbsnews.com/news/us-charges-sibling-leaders-mexico-cartel-8-million-reward/","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0104"],"verified":false},{"id":"claudia_sheinbaum","name":"Claudia Sheinbaum Pardo","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1962,"died":0,"role":"President of Mexico since October 2024, overseeing a sharp shift toward confronting cartels","status_2026":"Serving as President of Mexico","bio":"Born 24 June 1962 in Mexico City, a physicist with a doctorate in energy engineering from UNAM who worked at Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory and contributed to IPCC reports. She served as environment secretary of the Federal District under López Obrador, as borough mayor of Tlalpan, and as head of government of Mexico City from 2018 to 2023. She won the presidency on 2 June 2024 with 59.76 percent of the vote, the largest share in Mexico's modern democratic era, and was inaugurated on 1 October 2024 as the country's first woman president. Appointing Omar García Harfuch as security secretary, she broke with 'abrazos, no balazos' in practice: constitutional reforms on 31 December 2024 expanded the security ministry's investigative powers and widened mandatory pre-trial detention. Under intense pressure from the Trump administration, which designated six Mexican cartels as Foreign Terrorist Organizations on 20 February 2025 and threatened tariffs, she deployed 10,000 National Guard troops to the northern border in February 2025 and authorised three mass transfers of cartel prisoners to the United States: 29 on 27 February 2025, 26 on 12 August 2025 and 37 on 20 January 2026. After the assassination of Uruapan mayor Carlos Manzo on 1 November 2025 she launched the Michoacán Plan for Peace and Justice. On 22 February 2026 Mexican forces killed El Mencho with US intelligence support, while she publicly refused US troops on Mexican soil, saying sovereignty is not for sale.","notable":["First woman president of Mexico, inaugurated 1 October 2024","Made Omar García Harfuch security secretary and reversed AMLO's non-confrontation doctrine","Sent 29, 26 and 37 cartel prisoners to the US in February 2025, August 2025 and January 2026","Deployed 10,000 National Guard troops to the border to avert Trump tariffs in February 2025","Oversaw the operation that killed El Mencho on 22 February 2026 while rejecting US intervention"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1332,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Claudia_Sheinbaum","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claudia_Sheinbaum","https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c8xkxr1en3zo"],"verified":false},{"id":"omar_garcia_harfuch","name":"Omar García Harfuch","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1982,"died":0,"role":"Secretary of Security and Civilian Protection since October 2024; architect of Mexico's anti-cartel offensive","status_2026":"Serving as federal Secretary of Security and Civilian Protection","bio":"Born 25 February 1982 in Cuernavaca, Morelos, into a family at the centre of Mexico's security establishment: his grandfather, General Marcelino García Barragán, was defence secretary during the 1968 Tlatelolco massacre, and his father, Javier García Paniagua, headed the Federal Security Directorate in the late 1970s. He joined the Federal Police in 2008, became state coordinator in Guerrero in 2012, moved to the federal Criminal Investigation Agency in 2015, and was appointed Mexico City police chief on 4 October 2019 by then-mayor Claudia Sheinbaum. On 26 June 2020 CJNG gunmen ambushed his armoured vehicle on Paseo de la Reforma with .50-calibre rifles, wounding him three times and killing two bodyguards and a bystander; fourteen attackers were arrested and twelve later convicted. He publicly blamed the CJNG within hours. He joined Morena in 2023, won a Senate seat in 2024 but declined it, and on 1 October 2024 became federal Secretary of Security and Civilian Protection. His tenure has produced record fentanyl and methamphetamine seizures, tens of thousands of arrests, and the intelligence-led operations that killed El Mencho in Tapalpa on 22 February 2026 and captured CJNG second-in-command Audias Flores Silva, 'El Jardinero', in late April 2026. Critics continue to raise unproven allegations about his Guerrero-era record, including Ayotzinapa, which he denies.","notable":["Grandson of a defence secretary and son of a Federal Security Directorate chief","Survived a CJNG assassination attempt in Mexico City on 26 June 2020","Mexico City police chief 2019-2023, then federal security secretary from 1 October 2024","Directed the February 2026 operation in Tapalpa that killed El Mencho","Oversaw the capture of 'El Jardinero' in Nayarit in late April 2026"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1332,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omar_Garc%C3%ADa_Harfuch","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Claudia_Sheinbaum","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_in_Mexico"],"verified":false},{"id":"salvador_cienfuegos_zepeda","name":"Salvador Cienfuegos Zepeda","alias":"El Padrino (per US indictment)","cartels":[],"born":1948,"died":0,"role":"Mexican Secretary of National Defense 2012-2018; arrested and then freed by the United States in 2020","status_2026":"Free in Mexico; US charges dropped November 2020 and Mexican investigation closed January 2021","bio":"Born 14 June 1948, a career army officer who served as Secretary of National Defense from 1 December 2012 to 30 November 2018 under President Enrique Peña Nieto, commanding Mexico's army during the most militarised phase of the drug war. On 15 October 2020 US Drug Enforcement Administration agents arrested him at Los Angeles International Airport on charges of drug trafficking and money laundering. A Brooklyn indictment alleged that, using the code name 'El Padrino', he had protected the H-2 Cartel of Nayarit, tipping off traffickers about operations and steering the military against their rivals; the case rested largely on intercepted BlackBerry messages. His arrest, made without warning to Mexico City, provoked a furious response: President López Obrador's government threatened to expel DEA agents and curtail security cooperation. On 18 November 2020 US Attorney General William Barr moved to dismiss the charges 'in the interests of foreign policy', and Cienfuegos was flown home. On 14 January 2021 Mexico's Attorney General's Office announced it had exonerated him entirely and published the US evidence file, an act Washington called a breach of trust. Mexico then passed a law in December 2020 restricting foreign agents' operations on its territory. He retired from active service but continued in a federal advisory role, and no charges have been brought against him in either country since.","notable":["Defence secretary throughout the Peña Nieto administration, 2012-2018","Arrested at Los Angeles International Airport on 15 October 2020","US charges dismissed on 18 November 2020 after Mexico threatened to expel the DEA","Exonerated by Mexico's Attorney General on 14 January 2021","His case prompted Mexico's December 2020 law restricting foreign agents"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1332,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salvador_Cienfuegos_Zepeda","https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny","https://www.reuters.com/article/us-mexico-usa-cienfuegos-idUSKBN27Y2QW"],"verified":false},{"id":"carlos_manzo","name":"Carlos Alberto Manzo Rodríguez","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1985,"died":2025,"role":"Independent mayor of Uruapan, Michoacán, assassinated for confronting the cartels","status_2026":"Assassinated 1 November 2025 in Uruapan; a suspect in his killing was arrested 30 July 2026","bio":"Born 9 April 1985, he was a former Morena federal deputy who broke with the party and won the mayoralty of Uruapan, Michoacán, as an independent in the June 2024 elections at the head of the Movimiento del Sombrero, taking office on 1 September 2024. In a city at the centre of the avocado trade and squeezed between the CJNG and Cárteles Unidos, he made confrontation with organised crime his defining stance, personally accompanying police operations, publicising extortion of growers and packing plants, and repeatedly demanding federal reinforcements. He rejected President Sheinbaum's inherited security approach, declaring there could be no hugs for criminals, and warned publicly that he expected to be killed. On the evening of 1 November 2025, during the Festival de las Velas in Uruapan's central plaza on the Day of the Dead, a gunman shot him seven times at close range at about 8:10 p.m.; he died of abdominal wounds at the age of 40. His killing triggered the largest protests of Sheinbaum's presidency, with 70,000 to 80,000 people marching in Uruapan on 7 November 2025 and a national Generación Z movement adopting his hat as its symbol. The government announced the Michoacán Plan for Peace and Justice on 4 November 2025, and his widow Grecia Quiroz was sworn in as mayor on 5 November. On 30 July 2026 authorities arrested Ramón Ángel Álvarez Ayala, 'R1', as a suspect in the murder.","notable":["Independent mayor of Uruapan from 1 September 2024, leader of the Movimiento del Sombrero","Publicly rejected 'hugs not bullets' and predicted his own assassination","Shot seven times on 1 November 2025 during Uruapan's Day of the Dead festival","His murder triggered nationwide protests and the Michoacán Plan of 4 November 2025","Widow Grecia Quiroz succeeded him as mayor on 5 November 2025"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.4114,"lng":-102.0556,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlos_Manzo","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_in_Mexico","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Claudia_Sheinbaum"],"verified":false},{"id":"donald_trump","name":"Donald J. Trump","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1946,"died":0,"role":"US President whose second term designated Mexican cartels as Foreign Terrorist Organizations","status_2026":"Serving as President of the United States","bio":"Born 14 June 1946 in New York City, he returned to the presidency on 20 January 2025 and immediately made Mexican cartels a central national-security target. On inauguration day he signed an executive order directing the designation of cartels as foreign terrorist organizations and specially designated global terrorists. On 20 February 2025 the State Department formally designated six Mexican organisations, the Sinaloa Cartel, the Cártel Jalisco Nueva Generación, Cárteles Unidos, the Cártel del Noreste, the Gulf Cartel and La Nueva Familia Michoacana, together with MS-13 and Tren de Aragua; Canada matched the listings the next day. He paired the designations with tariff threats over fentanyl, which President Sheinbaum defused on 3 February 2025 by deploying 10,000 National Guard troops to the northern border, and a further 10 percent tariff followed on 24 July 2026. The designations underpinned an aggressive posture: Operation Southern Spear strikes on alleged drug boats in the Caribbean, the November 2025 designation of the Venezuelan Cartel de los Soles, and NSPM-7. On 8 January 2026 he publicly threatened to begin striking targets on land in Mexico, and in May 2026 CNN reported CIA operations inside Mexico, which both governments denied involved lethal action. Sheinbaum refused US troops on Mexican soil, but Mexico accepted intelligence support, which Reuters reported aided the 22 February 2026 operation that killed El Mencho.","notable":["Signed an executive order on 20 January 2025 directing cartel terrorist designations","State Department designated six Mexican cartels as FTOs on 20 February 2025","Used tariff threats to extract a 10,000-troop Mexican border deployment in February 2025","Threatened strikes on land inside Mexico on 8 January 2026","US intelligence support contributed to the February 2026 operation that killed El Mencho"],"state":"US","lat":38.9072,"lng":-77.0369,"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_State_list_of_Foreign_Terrorist_Organizations","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Donald_Trump","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Claudia_Sheinbaum"],"verified":false},{"id":"javier_valdez_cardenas","name":"Javier Valdez Cárdenas","alias":"","cartels":[],"born":1967,"died":2017,"role":"Investigative journalist and co-founder of Ríodoce, murdered in Culiacán in 2017","status_2026":"Murdered 15 May 2017; two gunmen convicted, alleged mastermind never tried in Mexico","bio":"Born 14 April 1967 in Culiacán, Sinaloa. He became the foremost chronicler of the human cost of the drug trade in his home state, writing for La Jornada and, in 2003, co-founding the weekly Ríodoce, which reported on organised crime and official corruption when almost no one else in Sinaloa would. His books, including Miss Narco, Los morros del narco and Huérfanos del narco, focused on the women, children and ordinary people consumed by the trafficking economy rather than on capos. He won the Committee to Protect Journalists' International Press Freedom Award in 2011. In February 2017 Ríodoce published an interview with Dámaso López Núñez, 'El Licenciado', then at war with El Chapo's sons for control of the Sinaloa Cartel; the sons were reportedly enraged. On 15 May 2017, around midday, gunmen forced Valdez from his car metres from the Ríodoce offices in Culiacán and shot him twelve times, taking his laptop and phone. He was 50. Heriberto Picos Barraza, 'El Koala', was sentenced to 14 years and 8 months in February 2020; Juan Francisco Picos Barrueta, 'El Quillo', received 32 years on 17 June 2021. A third suspect was found murdered in Sonora in September 2017. On 24 January 2020 Mexican prosecutors named Dámaso López Serrano, 'Mini Lic', as the intellectual author, but he was already in US custody and Mexico never obtained him.","notable":["Co-founded the Sinaloa weekly Ríodoce in 2003","Won the CPJ International Press Freedom Award in 2011","Shot twelve times outside his newsroom in Culiacán on 15 May 2017","Two gunmen convicted, sentenced to 14 years in 2020 and 32 years in 2021","Alleged mastermind 'Mini Lic' named in January 2020 but never tried in Mexico"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.8091,"lng":-107.394,"sources":["https://cpj.org/data/people/javier-valdez-cardenas/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Javier_Valdez_C%C3%A1rdenas","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Javier_Valdez_C%C3%A1rdenas"],"verified":false},{"id":"juan_carlos_valencia_gonzalez","name":"Juan Carlos Valencia González","alias":"El 03","cartels":["cjng"],"born":1984,"died":0,"role":"US-born stepson of El Mencho; commander of CJNG's Grupo Élite and, since 2026, the cartel's overall leader","status_2026":"Fugitive; named overall CJNG leader by the US National Counterterrorism Center on 8 July 2026; $5 million US reward outstanding","bio":"Born 12 September 1984 in Santa Ana, California, Valencia González holds both US and Mexican citizenship, making him the first American national credibly identified as the head of a major Mexican cartel. He is the son of Armando Valencia Cornelio, a founder of the Milenio Cartel, and of Rosalinda González Valencia of the Los Cuinis network; when his mother married Nemesio Oseguera Cervantes he became El Mencho's stepson and half-brother to Rubén Oseguera González. US authorities have tracked him since at least 2007. His standing inside the CJNG rose sharply after the 2015 arrest of El Menchito, when he absorbed part of his half-brother's portfolio, and he is credited with founding and running the Grupo Élite, also branded Fuerzas Especiales Mencho, the enforcement wing that spearheaded CJNG's campaigns in Guanajuato and central Mexico. A federal grand jury in the District of Columbia indicted him in October 2020 on drug trafficking and weapons charges, and the State Department posted a $5 million reward on 1 December 2021. After El Mencho was killed in February 2026 he emerged from a crowded field of contenders, and on 8 July 2026 the US National Counterterrorism Center's updated Counterterrorism Guide listed him as CJNG's leader, with Julio Alberto Castillo Rodríguez 'El Chorro' and Gonzalo Mendoza Gaytán 'El Sapo' as principal lieutenants.","notable":["Born 12 September 1984 in Santa Ana, California; a US citizen","Son of Milenio Cartel founder Armando Valencia Cornelio and Rosalinda González Valencia","Founded and commanded CJNG's Grupo Élite, also called Fuerzas Especiales Mencho","Indicted in the US District Court for the District of Columbia in October 2020","US State Department posted a $5 million reward on 1 December 2021","Identified by the US National Counterterrorism Center as CJNG's overall leader on 8 July 2026"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/juan-carlos-valencia-gonzalez-alias-03/","https://politico.mx/2026/07/08/eua-identifica-a-juan-carlos-valencia-03-como-nuevo-lider-del-cjng-tras-la-muerte-del-mencho/","https://insightcrime.org/news/us-names-el-mencho-successr-cjng-other-plans/","https://www.aa.com.tr/en/americas/el-mencho-s-us-born-stepson-emerges-to-lead-one-of-mexicos-most-dangerous-cartels-report/3872432"],"verified":false},{"id":"audias_flores_silva","name":"Audias Flores Silva","alias":"El Jardinero","cartels":["cjng"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"CJNG regional commander and head of El Mencho's personal security; leading succession contender in 2026","status_2026":"In Mexican custody; captured 27 April 2026 in Nayarit and bound over for trial (vinculado a proceso) on 5 May 2026; US reward raised to $15 million","bio":"Flores Silva rose through the CJNG as a plaza boss and eventually as head of security for Nemesio Oseguera Cervantes, a position that put him at the centre of the cartel's protection apparatus in the Jalisco-Nayarit corridor. On 13 August 2020 he was charged in US federal court with conspiring to manufacture and distribute five kilograms or more of cocaine and one kilogram or more of heroin for importation into the United States. In Mexico he repeatedly evaded justice through injunctions and unexecuted sentences, a record that made his eventual capture a political priority. After El Mencho was killed on 22 February 2026, Mexican and US officials named Flores Silva as the leading candidate to inherit the organisation. Following roughly 380 intelligence operations and 48 hours of aerial surveillance, some 120 Mexican Navy special forces troops, backed by helicopters, fixed-wing aircraft and around 400 additional personnel, closed on a cabin in the El Mirador area of Nayarit about 20 kilometres north of Puerto Vallarta at dawn on 27 April 2026. Marines found him hiding in a roadside drainage ditch and pulled him out without firing a shot. He was bound over for trial on 5 May 2026, and a US superseding indictment on 13 May 2026 added methamphetamine and money-laundering conspiracy counts.","notable":["Served as head of personal security for El Mencho and as a CJNG regional commander","Charged in US federal court on 13 August 2020 with cocaine and heroin importation conspiracy","Captured 27 April 2026 by Mexican Navy special forces in El Mirador, Nayarit, hiding in a drainage ditch","Bound over for trial in Mexico on 5 May 2026 as a CJNG plaza boss and leader","US superseding indictment of 13 May 2026 added methamphetamine trafficking and money laundering counts","US State Department reward increased to up to $15 million"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6534,"lng":-105.2253,"sources":["https://www.cnn.com/2026/04/29/americas/mexico-jardinero-jalisco-cartel-latam-intl","https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/mexican-navy-cjng-commander-el-jardinero/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/05/05/vinculan-a-proceso-a-audias-flores-el-jardinero-jefe-de-plaza-del-cjng/","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/28/mexicos-jalisco-drug-cartel-commander-el-jardinero-found-hiding-in-ditch"],"verified":false},{"id":"antonio_oseguera_cervantes","name":"Antonio Oseguera Cervantes","alias":"Tony Montana","cartels":["cjng","milenio"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Brother of El Mencho; CJNG logistics, weapons procurement and finance operator","status_2026":"In US custody in Washington, DC; pleaded guilty 31 July 2026 to cocaine, methamphetamine and firearms conspiracy counts, sentencing set for 13 November 2026","bio":"A native of Michoacán and roughly 67 years old at the time of his guilty plea, Antonio Oseguera Cervantes trafficked cocaine and methamphetamine into the United States for Mexican cartels for more than two decades. From about 2010 he worked directly under his younger brother Nemesio, handling logistics, weapons procurement and money movement for the CJNG, and coordinating closely with the Los Cuinis network that financed the organisation. Mexican authorities arrested him in December 2022 in the Guadalajara metropolitan area, in an operation officials described as a significant blow to CJNG's support structure. He was extradited to the United States in February 2025 and prosecuted in the US District Court for the District of Columbia, the same court that handled his nephew Rubén Oseguera González. Facing a mandatory minimum of 15 years and a maximum of two consecutive life terms, he reached a plea agreement that spared him a trial. On 31 July 2026, five months after his brother was killed in Tapalpa, he pleaded guilty to conspiring to distribute more than 450 kilograms of cocaine and more than 45 kilograms of methamphetamine destined for the United States, and to using, carrying and possessing a firearm in furtherance of that conspiracy. Judge Beryl Howell scheduled sentencing for 13 November 2026.","notable":["Younger brother of CJNG founder Nemesio 'El Mencho' Oseguera Cervantes","Arrested in December 2022 in the Guadalajara metropolitan area","Extradited to the United States in February 2025","Pleaded guilty on 31 July 2026 to trafficking more than 450 kg of cocaine and over 45 kg of methamphetamine, plus a firearms count","Faces a 15-year mandatory minimum and up to two consecutive life terms; sentencing set for 13 November 2026"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/brother-notorious-mexican-cartel-leader-pleads-guilty-international-drug-trafficking-and","https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2026/aug/3/cjng-cartel-leaders-brother-pleads-guilty-drug-trafficking-gun-charge/","https://abcnews.com/US/wireStory/man-slain-brother-led-mexican-cartel-pleads-guilty-135272290","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antonio_Oseguera_Cervantes"],"verified":false},{"id":"ivan_archivaldo_guzman_salazar","name":"Iván Archivaldo Guzmán Salazar","alias":"El Chapito","cartels":["sinaloa","chapitos"],"born":1983,"died":0,"role":"Eldest surviving son of El Chapo and senior leader of Los Chapitos","status_2026":"Fugitive in Sinaloa; $10 million US reward maintained through 2026; reportedly exploring a negotiated surrender to US authorities","bio":"Born 2 October 1983 in Culiacán, Sinaloa, to Joaquín Guzmán Loera and Alejandrina Salazar Hernández, Iván Archivaldo assumed control of his father's faction of the Sinaloa Cartel after El Chapo's 2016 recapture and 2017 extradition, sharing command with his brothers Jesús Alfredo, Ovidio and Joaquín as Los Chapitos. Under their leadership the faction industrialised fentanyl production in Sinaloa and became the principal driver of the US overdose crisis, according to US prosecutors. In April 2023 federal grand juries in the Southern District of New York and the Northern District of Illinois returned indictments charging him with continuing criminal enterprise, fentanyl importation and trafficking conspiracies, machine-gun and destructive-device possession and money laundering. Since the July 2024 abduction of Ismael Zambada García by his half-brother Joaquín, Iván Archivaldo has fought a grinding war against the Mayiza faction that has devastated Culiacán. His position deteriorated sharply in 2025 and 2026: Ovidio and Joaquín both pleaded guilty and agreed to cooperate with US prosecutors, dozens of his operators were captured, and in May 2026 the Treasury sanctioned a crypto-based laundering network tied to the group. He turned 43 in August 2026 still at large, with a $10 million ICE reward outstanding and reporting suggesting he and Jesús Alfredo were probing terms for a negotiated surrender.","notable":["Born 2 October 1983 in Culiacán, Sinaloa; son of Joaquín 'El Chapo' Guzmán Loera","Indicted in April 2023 in both the Southern District of New York and Northern District of Illinois","Carries a US reward of up to $10 million, publicised by ICE through 2026","Leads Los Chapitos in the civil war against the Mayiza faction that began after El Mayo's July 2024 capture","Los Angeles Times reporting in 2026 described contacts with US authorities over a possible negotiated surrender"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.8091,"lng":-107.394,"sources":["https://www.ice.gov/most-wanted/guzman-salazar-ivan","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/los-chapitos/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/08/14/ivan-archivaldo-guzman-cumple-43-anos-mientras-sigue-profugo-con-una-recompensa-de-eeuu-por-10-mdd-y-los-chapitos-atravesando-su-peor-momento/","https://www.nbclosangeles.com/news/local/sinaloa-cartel-chapito-reward-arrest/3875929/"],"verified":false},{"id":"jesus_alfredo_guzman_salazar","name":"Jesús Alfredo Guzmán Salazar","alias":"El Alfredillo","cartels":["sinaloa","chapitos"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Son of El Chapo and co-leader of Los Chapitos alongside his brother Iván Archivaldo","status_2026":"Fugitive in Sinaloa; $10 million US State Department reward; reportedly in contact with US authorities about a negotiated surrender","bio":"The second of Joaquín Guzmán Loera's sons by Alejandrina Salazar Hernández, Jesús Alfredo has operated as Iván Archivaldo's closest partner in running Los Chapitos since their father's extradition in January 2017. He came to public attention in August 2016 when CJNG gunmen kidnapped him from a Puerto Vallarta restaurant, an abduction resolved by negotiation within days and widely read as the opening of a lasting CJNG-Sinaloa hostility. In April 2023 a federal grand jury in the Southern District of New York indicted him for continuing criminal enterprise, fentanyl importation conspiracy, fentanyl trafficking conspiracy and related counts, part of the sweeping US case that first exposed the Chapitos' fentanyl laboratories, chemical supply chains from China and their use of extreme violence against rivals and informants. The State Department offers up to $10 million for information leading to his arrest or conviction. Since July 2024 he has been a principal commander in the Sinaloa civil war against the Mayiza, a conflict that has driven Culiacán's homicide rate to record levels and drawn heavy federal deployments. With Ovidio and Joaquín Guzmán López in US custody and cooperating, he and Iván Archivaldo are the last Chapitos at liberty; Mexican and US reporting in 2026 described both brothers as exploring terms for a negotiated surrender, though nothing has been confirmed.","notable":["Kidnapped by CJNG gunmen from a Puerto Vallarta restaurant in August 2016 and released after negotiations","Indicted in April 2023 by a federal grand jury in the Southern District of New York on fentanyl and continuing criminal enterprise charges","US State Department reward of up to $10 million","One of only two Chapitos brothers still at large as of September 2026","Reported in 2026 to be in indirect contact with US authorities over a possible surrender"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.8091,"lng":-107.394,"sources":["https://www.state.gov/jesus-alfredo-guzman-salazar","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/07/26/los-ultimos-chapitos-en-pie-ivan-archivaldo-y-jesus-alfredo-guzman-los-herederos-de-el-chapo-que-siguen-profugos/","https://www.elimparcial.com/mundo/2026/05/11/los-lideres-de-los-chapitos-ivan-archivaldo-y-jesus-alfredo-guzman-salazar-mantienen-conversaciones-con-autoridades-de-estados-unidos-para-negociar-su-rendicion-pactada/","https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/los-chapitos/"],"verified":false},{"id":"joaquin_guzman_lopez","name":"Joaquín Guzmán López","alias":"El Güero Moreno","cartels":["sinaloa","chapitos"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Son of El Chapo; abducted Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada and delivered him to US agents in July 2024","status_2026":"In US custody in Chicago; pleaded guilty 2 December 2025 and admitted kidnapping El Mayo; awaiting sentencing after a 1 June 2026 status hearing","bio":"The youngest of the four Chapitos and a son of Joaquín Guzmán Loera and Griselda López Pérez, Joaquín Guzmán López carried out the single most consequential act in modern Mexican organised crime. On 25 July 2024 he lured Ismael Zambada García to a meeting near Culiacán by claiming he needed help mediating a political dispute, then had armed men zip-tie the veteran capo, hood him, drive him to an airstrip and force him onto a private plane. Zambada was drugged for the flight; both men landed at a private airfield near El Paso, Texas, where US agents arrested them. The abduction shattered the Sinaloa Cartel's decades-old equilibrium and touched off the Chapitos-Mayiza civil war that has killed thousands in Sinaloa since September 2024. Guzmán López initially pleaded not guilty in Chicago. On 2 December 2025 he changed his plea in the US District Court for the Northern District of Illinois, admitting one count of drug conspiracy and one count of engaging in a continuing criminal enterprise, offences carrying a ten-year mandatory minimum and a maximum of life. His written plea agreement set out the kidnapping in detail, the first authoritative account of how El Mayo was taken. Judge Sharon Johnson Coleman ordered him back for a status hearing on 1 June 2026 ahead of sentencing.","notable":["Lured and abducted Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada on 25 July 2024, delivering him to US agents near El Paso, Texas","His action triggered the Sinaloa Cartel civil war between Los Chapitos and La Mayiza","Pleaded guilty in Chicago on 2 December 2025 to drug conspiracy and continuing criminal enterprise","Admitted in his plea agreement to zip-tying, hooding and sedating Zambada before the flight","Faces a ten-year mandatory minimum and up to life; status hearing held 1 June 2026 ahead of sentencing"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.8091,"lng":-107.394,"sources":["https://www.justice.gov/usao-ndil/pr/joaquin-guzman-lopez-son-el-chapo-and-head-sinaloa-cartel-pleads-guilty-federal-drug","https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/son-el-chapo-joaquin-guzman-lopez-admits-kidnapping-el-mayo/","https://www.npr.org/2025/12/02/nx-s1-5628200/plea-son-drug-kingpin-el-chapo-sinaloa-capo","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/11/29/son-of-jailed-mexican-drug-lord-el-chapo-to-plead-guilty-in-us-court"],"verified":false},{"id":"fausto_isidro_meza_flores","name":"Fausto Isidro Meza Flores","alias":"El Chapo Isidro","cartels":["beltran_leyva"],"born":1982,"died":0,"role":"Leader of the Meza Flores Organization, or Guasave Cartel, the main non-Sinaloa-Cartel power in northern Sinaloa","status_2026":"At large; reports of his capture at Guasave on 3 November 2025 were denied by Governor Rubén Rocha Moya and never confirmed","bio":"From Guasave in northern Sinaloa, Meza Flores entered trafficking in the 1990s under Amado Carrillo Fuentes of the Juárez Cartel and then joined the Beltrán Leyva Organization around 2000. When the Beltrán Leyvas split from the Sinaloa Cartel in 2008 he inherited their northern Sinaloa remnant, building the Meza Flores Organization, known locally as the Guasave Cartel, into a durable rival of the Guzmán and Zambada networks with routes through Sonora into Arizona. The US Treasury sanctioned him under the Kingpin Act in 2013, and the State Department posted a $5 million reward in 2017. Mexican authorities linked his organisation to a record seizure of 20 million counterfeit fentanyl pills in December 2024. On 4 February 2025 the FBI added him to its Ten Most Wanted Fugitives list. The collapse of the Sinaloa Cartel into civil war after July 2024 gave him room to expand, and by early 2026 analysts described him as a resurgent power positioned to profit from the Chapitos-Mayiza war, with unconfirmed reporting of tactical alignment with the Mayiza. On 3 November 2025 a federal operation in Guasave left 13 gunmen dead, four detained and nine kidnapping victims freed; several outlets reported he had been captured, but Governor Rubén Rocha Moya stated he was not among the detainees and no arrest was ever confirmed.","notable":["Began under the Juárez Cartel in the 1990s, then joined the Beltrán Leyva Organization around 2000","Sanctioned by the US Treasury under the Kingpin Act in 2013; $5 million State Department reward posted in 2017","Added to the FBI Ten Most Wanted Fugitives list on 4 February 2025","His organisation was linked to a record December 2024 seizure of 20 million counterfeit fentanyl pills","Reported captured at Guasave on 3 November 2025 in an operation that killed 13 gunmen, but Governor Rocha Moya denied he was detained"],"state":"SIN","lat":25.5667,"lng":-108.4667,"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/fausto-isidro-meza-flores-chapo-isidro/","https://www.state.gov/fausto-isidro-meza-flores","https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/2025/11/3/fuerzas-federales-abaten-13-presuntos-criminales-en-guasave-zona-de-influencia-del-chapo-isidro-362076.html","https://paralosdeapie.com.mx/2025/11/04/chapo-isidro-no-fue-detenido-informa-ruben-rocha/"],"verified":false},{"id":"rodrigo_paez_quintero","name":"Rodrigo Omar Páez Quintero","alias":"El R","cartels":["caborca","sonora"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Nephew of Rafael Caro Quintero and operational leader of the Caborca Cartel in Sonora","status_2026":"In US custody in Arizona; extradited July 2024 and awaiting trial in the District of Arizona on trafficking charges carrying up to life","bio":"A nephew of Rafael Caro Quintero, Páez Quintero rose within the family networks known as Los Páez or Los Paleteros that formed the armed core of the Caborca Cartel, the organisation Caro Quintero rebuilt in Sonora after his 2013 release from a Mexican prison. His peak came between 2017 and 2019, when he directed the organisation's trafficking through the Sonoran desert corridor toward Arizona and fought Los Chapitos for control of the Caborca and Altar valley routes, a dispute that made the state one of Mexico's most violent. Mexican federal agents and Navy personnel arrested him on 30 April 2023 in the Guadalajara metropolitan area. He was extradited to the United States in July 2024 and made his initial appearance on 16 July 2024 in the US District Court for the District of Arizona in Tucson, charged with conspiracy to possess with intent to distribute heroin, methamphetamine and fentanyl, plus multiple substantive possession counts, each carrying a maximum of life imprisonment and a $10 million fine. As of September 2026 no plea or sentencing had been publicly reported. The Caborca Cartel has continued to operate under other commanders, still contesting Sonora's border corridors with the Sinaloa Cartel's Chapitos faction.","notable":["Nephew of Guadalajara Cartel co-founder Rafael Caro Quintero","Led Caborca Cartel drug operations at their height between 2017 and 2019","Arrested 30 April 2023 in the Guadalajara metropolitan area by the FGR and the Navy","Extradited to the United States and first appeared in federal court in Tucson, Arizona, on 16 July 2024","Faces trafficking counts for heroin, methamphetamine and fentanyl each carrying a maximum of life"],"state":"SON","lat":30.7167,"lng":-112.1597,"sources":["https://www.justice.gov/usao-az/pr/lead-defendant-long-running-drug-trafficking-conspiracy-extradited-united-states-mexico","https://www.latintimes.com/top-mexican-cartel-member-extradited-us-face-drug-conspiracy-charges-556421","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2023/04/30/cartel-de-caborca-cayo-el-r-quien-disputaba-plaza-con-los-chapitos/","https://politico.mx/2025/11/21/el-cartel-de-caborca-la-dinastia-quintero-que-se-apropio-de-la-perla-del-desierto/"],"verified":false},{"id":"juan_jose_farias_alvarez","name":"Juan José Farías Álvarez","alias":"El Abuelo","cartels":["autodefensas","carteles_unidos"],"born":1970,"died":0,"role":"Former autodefensa commander turned founder of the Tepalcatepec Cartel and leader of Cárteles Unidos in Michoacán","status_2026":"Fugitive in Michoacán; $10 million US reward posted 14 August 2025; his son and nephew indicted in the US in July 2026","bio":"Born 10 August 1970 in Tepalcatepec, Michoacán, Farías Álvarez emerged in 2013-2014 as one of the most prominent commanders of the Michoacán autodefensa movement that drove the Caballeros Templarios from Tierra Caliente, even as authorities and journalists documented his earlier trafficking record. As the self-defence movement was co-opted and fragmented, he converted his militia into a criminal organisation: in 2019 he founded the Tepalcatepec Cartel and knitted together a coalition of Michoacán groups as Cárteles Unidos, whose central purpose was to block the CJNG's advance into the avocado and lime economies of Tierra Caliente. The resulting front line around Tepalcatepec, Aguililla and Buenavista has been among the most militarised in Mexico, featuring trench systems, improvised armoured vehicles and weaponised drones. On 14 August 2025 the State Department's Bureau of International Narcotics and Law Enforcement Affairs offered up to $10 million for information leading to his arrest or conviction. Pressure intensified in 2026: in April a major Tierra Caliente operation aimed at him instead detained his son Víctor; his operator Alfonso Valencia 'El Repollo' was arrested on 29 May 2026; and in early July 2026 the US Justice Department indicted his son Juan José Farías Mendoza and nephew Israel Vega Farías on drug trafficking and terrorism-support charges. He remained at large in September 2026.","notable":["Born 10 August 1970 in Tepalcatepec, Michoacán","Prominent commander in the 2013-2014 Michoacán autodefensa uprising against the Caballeros Templarios","Founded the Tepalcatepec Cartel in 2019 and leads the Cárteles Unidos coalition against the CJNG","US State Department posted a reward of up to $10 million on 14 August 2025","His son Víctor was detained in April 2026 and his son and nephew were indicted in the United States in July 2026"],"state":"MIC","lat":19.1833,"lng":-102.85,"sources":["https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/07/05/el-abuelo-farias-de-autodefensa-a-lider-de-carteles-unidos-la-torcida-vida-por-la-que-lo-busca-eeuu-junto-a-su-hijo-y-sobrino/","https://www.informador.mx/internacional/narcotrafico-estados-unidos-imputa-al-hijo-y-un-sobrino-de-el-abuelo-20260703-0016.html","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2026/04/18/iban-por-el-abuelo-farias-lider-de-carteles-unidos-megaoperativo-en-tierra-caliente-deja-un-detenido/","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Jos%C3%A9_Far%C3%ADas_%C3%81lvarez"],"verified":false},{"id":"johnny_hurtado_olascoaga","name":"Johnny Hurtado Olascoaga","alias":"El Pez","cartels":["familia_michoacana","nueva_familia_michoacana"],"born":1973,"died":0,"role":"Co-founder and top leader of La Nueva Familia Michoacana","status_2026":"Fugitive; indicted in the US on 24 September 2024, sanctioned by Treasury, with a $5 million State Department reward","bio":"Born 1 March 1973 in the Arcelia area of Guerrero, Hurtado Olascoaga and his brother José Alfredo rebuilt the remains of La Familia Michoacana into La Nueva Familia Michoacana around 2011, taking control of the Tierra Caliente triangle where Guerrero, the State of Mexico and Michoacán meet. The organisation is unusual among Mexican cartels for its territorial governance model: it taxes and administers dozens of municipalities, running extortion of farmers, transporters and businesses, illegal iron ore mining, logging and migrant smuggling alongside large-scale drug trafficking. The DEA estimates the group moves more than 36 tonnes of methamphetamine, 12 tonnes of heroin and 12 tonnes of cocaine into the United States each year and has publicised its distribution of brightly coloured 'rainbow fentanyl' pills. The Treasury sanctioned the organisation and its leaders in June 2024, and on 24 September 2024 the US Justice Department indicted both brothers on three counts including conspiracy to manufacture and distribute controlled substances. In April 2025 the State Department announced rewards totalling $8 million for the pair, $5 million of it for Johnny. The Trump administration's designation of Mexican cartels as foreign terrorist organisations brought further pressure, and in May 2026 the Justice Department intensified its campaign against the group, but as of September 2026 he had not been captured.","notable":["Born 1 March 1973 in Guerrero; co-founded La Nueva Familia Michoacana around 2011 with his brother","Organisation controls territory across Guerrero, the State of Mexico and Michoacán through extortion, illegal mining and drug trafficking","DEA attributes to the group more than 36 tonnes of methamphetamine and 12 tonnes each of heroin and cocaine annually","Sanctioned by the US Treasury in June 2024 and indicted in the United States on 24 September 2024","US State Department reward of up to $5 million announced in April 2025"],"state":"GRO","lat":18.3167,"lng":-100.2833,"sources":["https://www.state.gov/johnny-hurtado-olascoaga","https://www.cbsnews.com/news/us-charges-familia-michoacana-cartel-leaders-mexico-8-million-reward/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2025/04/16/quienes-son-johnny-y-jose-alfredo-hurtado-olascoaga-lideres-de-la-nueva-familia-michoacana-por-los-que-eeuu-ofrece-8-mdd/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Nueva_Familia_Michoacana_Organization"],"verified":false},{"id":"jose_alfredo_hurtado_olascoaga","name":"José Alfredo Hurtado Olascoaga","alias":"El Fresa","cartels":["familia_michoacana","nueva_familia_michoacana"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Co-founder and second-in-command of La Nueva Familia Michoacana","status_2026":"Fugitive; indicted in the US on 24 September 2024 with a $3 million State Department reward, still listed as wanted in April 2026","bio":"The younger of the two Hurtado Olascoaga brothers from the Arcelia area of Guerrero, José Alfredo has run La Nueva Familia Michoacana jointly with Johnny since roughly 2011 and is generally described by US authorities as the organisation's second in command. His profile is more public than most cartel leaders': after the October 2022 San Miguel Totolapan massacre in Guerrero, in which gunmen killed the mayor, his father and 18 others, he appeared in a video denying responsibility and blaming rivals, an example of the group's practice of communicating directly with the population it taxes. He is associated with the organisation's operations in the State of Mexico and Guerrero, including extortion of avocado, lime and cattle producers, illegal iron ore extraction and the movement of methamphetamine and fentanyl to the United States. The Treasury sanctioned him in June 2024 over the group's rainbow fentanyl distribution, and on 24 September 2024 the Justice Department indicted him alongside his brother and an Atlanta-based money launderer on three counts including drug manufacturing and distribution conspiracy. In April 2025 the State Department offered up to $3 million for information leading to his arrest. A State Department listing dated 9 April 2026 still described him as wanted, and Mexican federal deployments against the group in the State of Mexico and Morelos through mid-2026 did not produce his capture.","notable":["Co-leader of La Nueva Familia Michoacana with his brother Johnny 'El Pez' Hurtado Olascoaga","Publicly denied responsibility on video for the October 2022 San Miguel Totolapan massacre in Guerrero","Sanctioned by the US Treasury in June 2024 over rainbow fentanyl distribution","Indicted in the United States on 24 September 2024 with his brother and an Atlanta money launderer","US State Department reward of up to $3 million; still listed as wanted in April 2026"],"state":"GRO","lat":18.3167,"lng":-100.2833,"sources":["https://www.state.gov/jose-alfredo-hurtado-olascoaga","https://www.justice.gov/opa/video/leaders-la-nueva-familia-michoacana-and-atlanta-based-money-launderer-indicted","https://www.borderlandbeat.com/2022/10/san-miguel-totolapan-guerrero-johnny.html","https://www.publimetro.com.mx/noticias/2026/05/25/eeuu-intensifica-ofensiva-contra-la-nueva-familia-michoacana-y-va-por-el-pez-y-el-fresa/"],"verified":false},{"id":"jose_antonio_yepez_ortiz","name":"José Antonio Yépez Ortiz","alias":"El Marro","cartels":["santa_rosa_lima"],"born":1980,"died":0,"role":"Founder and leader of the Cártel de Santa Rosa de Lima, Mexico's most prominent fuel-theft organisation","status_2026":"Imprisoned in Mexico under a 60-year sentence; sanctioned by the US Treasury on 17 December 2025 for continuing to direct the cartel from prison","bio":"Born 23 July 1980 in Santa Cruz de Juventino Rosas, Guanajuato, Yépez built the Cártel de Santa Rosa de Lima out of the huachicolero networks that tapped Pemex pipelines around the Salamanca refinery, turning fuel theft into an industry and adding extortion, kidnapping and retail drug sales. From 2017 his war with the CJNG for control of Guanajuato made the state Mexico's deadliest by absolute homicides for several consecutive years, marked by narco-video threats, attacks on Pemex facilities and mass killings at rehabilitation centres and bars. He was arrested on 2 August 2020 by the army at a property in Juventino Rosas, and on 14 January 2022 a Guanajuato court sentenced him to 60 years for the aggravated kidnapping of a businesswoman. Prison did not end his role. On 17 December 2025 the US Treasury sanctioned the Cártel de Santa Rosa de Lima as a transnational criminal organisation and named Yépez as directing its operations from custody, passing instructions through relatives and lawyers, laundering proceeds and brokering alliances with the Sinaloa and Gulf cartels against the CJNG. Mexican reporting in January 2026 confirmed the same picture of remote command, making him one of the clearest modern examples of a Mexican capo whose imprisonment left his organisation intact.","notable":["Born 23 July 1980 in Santa Cruz de Juventino Rosas, Guanajuato","Built the Cártel de Santa Rosa de Lima around Pemex pipeline fuel theft near the Salamanca refinery","His war with the CJNG made Guanajuato Mexico's deadliest state by homicide count for several years","Arrested by the army on 2 August 2020 and sentenced to 60 years on 14 January 2022 for aggravated kidnapping","Sanctioned by the US Treasury on 17 December 2025, which said he still directs the cartel from prison"],"state":"GUA","lat":20.5175,"lng":-100.98,"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/jose-antonio-yepez-ortiz-alias-el-marro/","https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0339","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/01/27/desde-prision-el-marro-seguiria-dirigiendo-al-cartel-santa-rosa-de-lima/","https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/el-marro-sentenced-to-60-years/"],"verified":false},{"id":"juan_gerardo_trevino_chavez","name":"Juan Gerardo Treviño Chávez","alias":"El Huevo","cartels":["zetas","cdn"],"born":1982,"died":0,"role":"Leader of the Cartel del Noreste, the Zetas successor organisation based in Nuevo Laredo","status_2026":"In US custody; pleaded guilty to 15 counts on 19 November 2024 in San Antonio and faces up to life, with sentencing pending","bio":"A nephew of Los Zetas leaders Miguel Ángel and Óscar Omar Treviño Morales, and a US citizen by birth, Treviño Chávez took over the Cartel del Noreste in September 2016 and ran it from Nuevo Laredo, Tamaulipas. Under his command the CDN kept the Zetas' methods, including the CDN Tropa del Infierno enforcement unit, armoured vehicles and open firefights with Mexican forces, while trafficking marijuana, cocaine and methamphetamine across the Laredo border crossings and running extortion and migrant smuggling in Nuevo Laredo. Because he held US citizenship, Mexican authorities were able to expel rather than extradite him: he was captured in Nuevo Laredo on 13 March 2022 and handed to US agents within two days, an unusually rapid transfer that triggered days of CDN roadblocks, vehicle burnings and an attack on the US consulate in the city. He was prosecuted jointly by the Western and Eastern Districts of Texas. On 19 November 2024 he pleaded guilty in federal court in San Antonio to 15 counts covering drug trafficking conspiracies, money laundering and the employment of minors in drug operations, and faces a maximum of life imprisonment. Sentencing was initially scheduled for May 2025; no sentence had been publicly reported as of September 2026. The CDN has continued operating in Tamaulipas under other Treviño-linked commanders.","notable":["Nephew of Los Zetas leaders Miguel Ángel 'Z-40' and Óscar Omar 'Z-42' Treviño Morales","Led the Cartel del Noreste from September 2016 until his capture","Captured in Nuevo Laredo on 13 March 2022 and expelled to the United States within two days because he held US citizenship","His capture set off CDN roadblocks, arson and an attack on the US consulate in Nuevo Laredo","Pleaded guilty to 15 counts in San Antonio federal court on 19 November 2024, facing up to life"],"state":"TAM","lat":27.4763,"lng":-99.5164,"sources":["https://www.justice.gov/usao-wdtx/pr/us-attorneys-western-district-texas-and-eastern-district-texas-announce-former-cartel","https://www.kens5.com/article/news/crime/drug-cartel-leader-pleads-guilty-san-antonio/273-7650f2d5-729c-404d-bf01-97aa9dd5f466","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2024/11/20/estos-son-los-cargos-criminales-de-los-que-el-huevo-trevino-exlider-del-cartel-del-noreste-se-declaro-culpable/","https://www.ice.gov/news/releases/leader-cartel-del-noreste-arrested-following-ice-hsi-investigation"],"verified":false},{"id":"roberto_moyado_esparza","name":"Roberto Moyado Esparza","alias":"El Betito","cartels":["union_tepito"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Founder and leader of La Unión Tepito, the dominant criminal group in central Mexico City","status_2026":"Imprisoned in a Mexican federal prison serving 43 years and nine months; a 2025 appeal cut a separate concurrent sentence by six months","bio":"Moyado Esparza built La Unión Tepito out of the wholesale markets and tenements of the Tepito barrio in Mexico City's Cuauhtémoc borough, converting a local extortion racket into the capital's most powerful criminal organisation. Under his leadership the group controlled retail drug sales in the Centro Histórico, Condesa, Roma and Zona Rosa nightlife districts, extorted bars, market stalls and pirated-goods vendors, and moved into kidnapping and contract killing, with a documented supply relationship for cocaine and synthetic drugs from larger cartels. His arrest on 8 August 2018 in Mexico City was presented by authorities as the decapitation of the group and was followed by its fragmentation and a long war with the breakaway Anti-Unión Tepito that has driven much of the capital's homicide and disappearance violence since. In December 2022 a Mexico City court sentenced him to 43 years and nine months for a qualified homicide and attempted homicide committed on 1 March 2015 in the Colonia Morelos of the Venustiano Carranza borough; an appellate court confirmed that sentence in December 2023. In April 2025 a collegiate appeals court trimmed six months from a separate concurrent conviction for drug offences, firearms possession and possession of military-issue ammunition. Mexico City security officials say his family has continued to run parts of the organisation during his imprisonment.","notable":["Founded and led La Unión Tepito, Mexico City's dominant criminal organisation","Arrested on 8 August 2018 in Mexico City","Sentenced in December 2022 to 43 years and nine months for a March 2015 qualified homicide and attempted homicide","Sentence confirmed on appeal in December 2023; a separate concurrent sentence was reduced by six months in April 2025","His arrest fragmented La Unión Tepito and fuelled its long war with the breakaway Anti-Unión Tepito"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.445,"lng":-99.125,"sources":["https://www.proceso.com.mx/nacional/cdmx/2023/12/22/el-betito-de-la-union-tepito-es-sentenciado-43-anos-de-prision-por-homicidio-en-la-morelos-320844.html","https://www.milenio.com/policia/confirman-sentencia-carcel-betito-union-tepito","https://mvsnoticias.com/nacional/2025/4/15/tribunal-reduce-condena-el-betito-de-que-va-su-caso-687915.html","https://lasillarota.com/metropoli/2025/7/9/la-union-tepito-el-betito-sigue-liderando-desde-la-carcel-544786.html"],"verified":false},{"id":"hernan_bermudez_requena","name":"Hernán Bermúdez Requena","alias":"Comandante H","cartels":["barredora","cjng"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Former Tabasco security secretary accused of founding and leading La Barredora, a CJNG-aligned criminal group","status_2026":"In Mexican custody; prosecutors in Tabasco sought a cumulative 154-year sentence at an intermediate hearing in June 2026, with an oral trial pending","bio":"Bermúdez Requena served as Secretary of Public Security of Tabasco between 2019 and 2024, under governor Adán Augusto López Hernández, later interior minister and Morena's Senate leader. Prosecutors allege that while holding that office he founded and directed La Barredora, a criminal group aligned with the CJNG that ran extortion, kidnapping, fuel theft and drug trafficking across Tabasco and helped move CJNG product through the Gulf coast and into Central America. The case became one of the most damaging corruption scandals of the Sheinbaum era because of its proximity to the governing party's leadership. He fled Mexico on 26 January 2025, flying from Mérida to Panama and then, according to investigators, to Spain, Brazil and finally Paraguay. Interpol issued a red notice on 17 July 2025. Paraguayan police arrested him on 12 September 2025 in Asunción with cash, wine and jewellery; he refused simplified extradition, but Paraguay expelled him and he was flown to Mexico on 17 September 2025. In Tabasco he faces charges of criminal association, aggravated kidnapping and aggravated extortion in case 213/2025, and was separately bound over on 13 April 2026 for embezzlement and is under investigation for forced disappearance. At an intermediate hearing in June 2026 the state prosecutor presented 40 investigation files and requested a cumulative 154-year sentence.","notable":["Secretary of Public Security of Tabasco from 2019 to 2024 while allegedly leading La Barredora","Fled Mexico on 26 January 2025 via Panama, Spain and Brazil to Paraguay","Interpol red notice issued 17 July 2025; arrested in Paraguay on 12 September 2025","Expelled from Paraguay and returned to Mexico on 17 September 2025","Tabasco prosecutors requested a cumulative 154-year sentence at a June 2026 intermediate hearing"],"state":"TAB","lat":17.9895,"lng":-92.9475,"sources":["https://mexiconewsdaily.com/news/former-tabasco-security-minister-arrested-paraguay-organized-crime-charges/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/06/16/caso-hernan-bermudez-requena-que-sigue-en-su-proceso-luego-de-que-la-fiscalia-de-tabasco-solicito-mas-de-150-anos-de-prision/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2026/04/13/comandante-h-suma-delitos-vinculan-a-proceso-a-hernan-bermudez-ahora-por-peculado/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hern%C3%A1n_Berm%C3%BAdez_Requena"],"verified":false},{"id":"celso_ortega_jimenez","name":"Celso Ortega Jiménez","alias":"El Ardilla","cartels":["ardillos","zetas"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"Leader of Los Ardillos, the criminal group dominating the mountain region of central Guerrero","status_2026":"At large in Guerrero; wanted by the FGR but never captured, with his group's offensive in the Baja Montaña producing at least 76 killings since May 2026","bio":"Also known as La Vela or El Ardillo Mayor, Celso Ortega Jiménez is one of the sons of Celso Ortega Rosas, a former rural police officer and poppy grower from Quechultenango who founded Los Ardillos in the early 2000s; after the father's murder in 2011 the sons took over. Reporting has linked Ortega Jiménez to an earlier period with Los Zetas. Los Ardillos control opium production, extortion and transport routes across the Chilapa, Quechultenango, Zitlala and Tixtla corridor of central Guerrero, and have fought a long, exceptionally brutal war with Los Rojos and with the region's community police and Indigenous Nahua self-defence forces, punctuated by massacres, decapitations and mass displacement. The group has been repeatedly tied to state and municipal politics: a video released in August 2023 showed Ortega Jiménez meeting the then mayor of Chilpancingo, Norma Otilia Hernández, and his brother Bernardo Ortega Jiménez has served as a state deputy and president of the Guerrero congress. Through the first half of 2026 the organisation pushed east and north toward exits to Puebla and Morelos, intensifying violence in Olinalá, Tlapa, Alpoyeca and Huamuxtitlán. An offensive begun on 6 May 2026 in the Baja Montaña left at least 76 people dead and 25 disappeared, and used explosives dropped from drones, forcing the displacement of 800 to 1,000 people from Indigenous communities.","notable":["Son of Los Ardillos founder Celso Ortega Rosas, who was murdered in 2011","Reported to have been a member of Los Zetas before leading Los Ardillos","Filmed in August 2023 meeting the then mayor of Chilpancingo, Norma Otilia Hernández","His brother Bernardo Ortega Jiménez served as a Guerrero state deputy and congress president","An offensive begun 6 May 2026 in Guerrero's Baja Montaña left at least 76 dead and displaced up to 1,000 Indigenous residents"],"state":"GRO","lat":17.5972,"lng":-99.1786,"sources":["https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/ardillos/","https://www.infobae.com/mexico/2026/05/12/quienes-son-los-ardillos-historia-lideres-y-crimenes-del-grupo-que-desplaza-comunidades-indigenas-en-guerrero/","https://www.dallasnews.com/espanol/al-dia/mexico/2025/09/04/ardillos-guerrero-celso-ortega-narcos-terror-evelyn-salgado/","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/nacional/2024/02/18/quien-es-y-donde-se-mueve-celso-ortega-el-lider-de-los-ardillos-buscado-por-la-fgr/"],"verified":false},{"id":"ricardo_ruiz_velasco","name":"Ricardo Ruiz Velasco","alias":"El Doble R","cartels":["cjng"],"born":0,"died":0,"role":"CJNG regional boss and Grupo Élite commander; a leading contender to succeed El Mencho","status_2026":"In Mexican custody; captured 11 March 2026 in Mexicali, Baja California, three weeks after El Mencho's death","bio":"Known as El Doble R, El RR, El R2 and La Tripa, Ruiz Velasco was for years one of the CJNG's most important regional commanders and a commander of the Grupo Élite, the cartel's principal armed wing. He is associated with the CJNG's violent expansion into Jalisco's neighbouring states and with high-impact attacks, including the 2020 killing of Jalisco former governor Aristóteles Sandoval in Puerto Vallarta, in which Mexican investigators identified him as a planner. The US State Department has offered a reward for information leading to his arrest, and he features in US indictments of CJNG leadership. When Nemesio Oseguera Cervantes was killed on 22 February 2026, security analysts and narcocorridos alike named Ruiz Velasco as one of the strongest candidates to inherit the organisation, citing his territorial control, his operational role and his demonstrated capacity for orchestrating large-scale violence. That candidacy lasted seventeen days: on 11 March 2026 he was captured in Mexicali, Baja California, far from CJNG's Jalisco core. His removal, followed six weeks later by the capture of Audias Flores Silva in Nayarit, cleared two of the three leading contenders and helped consolidate the position of Juan Carlos Valencia González, whom US intelligence named as CJNG's leader in July 2026.","notable":["Commander of CJNG's Grupo Élite and a senior regional boss","Identified by Mexican investigators as a planner of the December 2020 killing of former Jalisco governor Aristóteles Sandoval","Named after El Mencho's death as one of the strongest candidates to lead the CJNG","Captured 11 March 2026 in Mexicali, Baja California","His capture, with that of El Jardinero in April 2026, cleared the way for Juan Carlos Valencia González"],"state":"JAL","lat":20.6597,"lng":-103.3496,"sources":["https://www.state.gov/ricardo-ruiz-velasco","https://lasillarota.com/guanajuato/estado/2026/3/12/capturan-el-doble-r-principal-heredero-de-el-mencho-590199.html","https://www.milenio.com/policia/el-doble-r-quien-es-el-lider-regional-del-cjng","https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ricardo_Ruiz_Velasco"],"verified":false},{"id":"andres_manuel_lopez_obrador","name":"Andrés Manuel López Obrador","alias":"AMLO","cartels":["government"],"born":1953,"died":0,"role":"President of Mexico 2018-2024; architect of the 'abrazos, no balazos' security policy","status_2026":"Retired at his ranch in Palenque, Chiapas; broke his silence with an open letter in June 2026 defending Sheinbaum and appealing to Donald Trump","bio":"Born 13 November 1953 in Tepetitán, Macuspana, Tabasco, López Obrador won the presidency in 2018 after two previous attempts and governed from 1 December 2018 to 30 September 2024. His security policy, summarised as 'abrazos, no balazos' (hugs, not bullets), rejected the confrontational kingpin strategy of his predecessors in favour of social programmes and non-engagement, while militarising civilian security through the new National Guard and expanding the armed forces' role in infrastructure and customs. Critics point to the October 2019 Culiacanazo, when his government released Ovidio Guzmán López to end a cartel assault on Culiacán, as the emblematic case. He dissolved the Federal Police, cut cooperation with the DEA after the 2020 arrest of General Salvador Cienfuegos in Los Angeles, and pushed through a law restricting foreign agents. Homicides remained near record levels for most of his term while disappearances rose. He faced repeated US reporting, denied by him as fabrication, about alleged Sinaloa Cartel contributions to his 2006 and 2018 campaigns; no formal US investigation was opened. He retired to his ranch in Palenque, Chiapas, and published books including Grandeza. In June 2026 he broke his silence with a long open letter defending President Claudia Sheinbaum amid US-Mexico tensions, later saying he would re-enter public life only if Mexican democracy or sovereignty were threatened.","notable":["President of Mexico from 1 December 2018 to 30 September 2024","Adopted the 'abrazos, no balazos' security doctrine and created the militarised National Guard","Ordered the release of Ovidio Guzmán López during the October 2019 Culiacanazo","Forced the return of General Salvador Cienfuegos from US custody in 2020 and curtailed DEA cooperation","Denied US press reports of alleged cartel funding of his campaigns; no formal US investigation was opened","Published an open letter from retirement in June 2026 defending Sheinbaum and addressing Donald Trump"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1332,"sources":["https://www.propublica.org/article/mexico-amlo-dea-probe-cartel-campaign-donations","https://theyucatantimes.com/2026/06/may-the-other-trump-return-amlo-reappears-with-open-letter-amid-u-s-mexico-tensions/","https://www.britannica.com/biography/Andres-Manuel-Lopez-Obrador","https://www.latintimes.com/mexican-democracy-under-threat-former-president-amlo-says-he-will-leave-retirement-defend-it-592113"],"verified":false},{"id":"omar_garcia_harfuch","name":"Omar Hamid García Harfuch","alias":"","cartels":["government"],"born":1982,"died":0,"role":"Mexico's Secretary of Security and Citizen Protection since October 2024; architect of the operations against El Mencho and Los Chapitos","status_2026":"In office; credited with the February 2026 Tapalpa operation and a wave of high-level captures, and widely seen as a 2030 presidential contender","bio":"Born 25 February 1982, García Harfuch is the grandson of Marcelino García Barragán, defence secretary during the 1968 Tlatelolco massacre, and a career federal police officer who worked in the Federal Police's intelligence divisions before becoming Mexico City's Secretary of Citizen Security in 2019 under Claudia Sheinbaum. On 26 June 2020 a CJNG hit squad of roughly 30 gunmen ambushed his armoured vehicle on Paseo de la Reforma with rifles and a .50 calibre weapon; he survived with three bullet wounds while two bodyguards and a bystander were killed, and he publicly blamed the CJNG within hours. Appointed federal Security Secretary on 1 October 2024, he built a specialised intelligence and investigation apparatus that has driven the sharpest sustained campaign against cartel leadership in Mexican history: mass seizures of fentanyl laboratories, the dismantling of money-laundering and port-corruption networks, the expulsion of 55 cartel figures to the United States across 2025, the April 2026 operation against El Guano in Durango, the March 2026 capture of Ricardo Ruiz Velasco, the April 2026 capture of Audias Flores Silva and, above all, the 22 February 2026 army operation in Tapalpa that killed the man who had tried to assassinate him six years earlier. His government reports a 37 percent fall in daily homicides.","notable":["Survived a CJNG assassination attempt on Paseo de la Reforma, Mexico City, on 26 June 2020","Mexico City Secretary of Citizen Security 2019-2023 under Claudia Sheinbaum","Federal Secretary of Security and Citizen Protection since 1 October 2024","Oversaw the expulsion of 55 cartel figures to the United States during 2025","Directed the security cabinet during the 22 February 2026 Tapalpa operation that killed El Mencho","Widely described as an early front-runner for the 2030 Mexican presidency"],"state":"CMX","lat":19.4326,"lng":-99.1332,"sources":["https://mexiconewsdaily.com/politics/who-is-omar-garcia-harfuch-mexicos-security-supremo/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omar_Garc%C3%ADa_Harfuch","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Jalisco_operation","https://www.elfinanciero.com.mx/estados/2026/04/22/harfuch-confirma-operativo-contra-el-guano-hermano-del-chapo-todavia-no-se-confirma-su-detencion/"],"verified":false},{"id":"ruben_rocha_moya","name":"Rubén Rocha Moya","alias":"","cartels":["government"],"born":1949,"died":0,"role":"Governor of Sinaloa since 2021; the first sitting Mexican governor indicted by the United States","status_2026":"Back in office in Sinaloa since 21 August 2026 after a 112-day leave, while under US indictment in the Southern District of New York","bio":"Born 15 June 1949 in Badiraguato, Sinaloa, a former teacher, university rector and long-time leftist politician, Rocha Moya won the Sinaloa governorship for Morena in 2021 and took office on 1 November 2021. His term has been dominated by the collapse of the Sinaloa Cartel's internal order: he was reportedly scheduled to meet Ismael Zambada García on the day of the 25 July 2024 abduction, and the war between Los Chapitos and La Mayiza that followed pushed Culiacán's homicide rate to record levels and drew tens of thousands of federal troops into the state. On 29 April 2026 federal prosecutors in the Southern District of New York indicted him and nine other current and former Mexican officials on drug and weapons trafficking charges, alleging that he met El Chapo's sons before the 2021 election, promised to install officials friendly to their operations, and that cartel members stole ballot boxes and intimidated opponents to secure his victory; in exchange, prosecutors say, he shielded the organisation from investigation and directed police to safeguard drug and weapons shipments. It was the first time the United States charged a sitting Mexican governor. He denied the allegations, took leave from 1 May 2026, and returned to office on 21 August 2026 after Mexico's Attorney General's Office said it lacked sufficient evidence for charges under Mexican law, with investigations reportedly still open.","notable":["Born 15 June 1949 in Badiraguato, Sinaloa; governor of Sinaloa since 1 November 2021","Reportedly due to meet Ismael Zambada García on 25 July 2024, the day Zambada was abducted","Indicted on 29 April 2026 in the Southern District of New York with nine other Mexican officials","First sitting Mexican governor ever charged by the United States","Took leave from 1 May 2026 and resumed office on 21 August 2026 after Mexico's FGR found insufficient evidence"],"state":"SIN","lat":24.8091,"lng":-107.394,"sources":["https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/governor-sinaloa-and-nine-other-current-and-former-mexican-officials-charged-drug","https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/8/21/mexico-governor-ruben-rocha-returns-to-office-despite-us-criminal-charges","https://insightcrime.org/news/insight-take-governor-alleged-sinaloa-cartel-ties-criminal-governance/","https://www.democracynow.org/2026/4/30/headlines/us_prosecutors_indict_sinaloa_governor_ruben_rocha_moya_and_nine_other_mexican_officials"],"verified":false}];
